var e=JSON.parse('[[501,"To improve the UX of a payment form it is worth adding the popular payment methods, such as credit cards, PayPal, Apple Pay, Google Pay and local methods. It is important to minimize the number of fields, to use autofill and to show security indicators. This reduces friction and increases conversion.","Improving the UX of a payment form\\nThe payment form is a critical stage of the sales funnel. Poor UX can lead to abandoned carts. The main aim is to make the process fast, secure and intuitive.\\nThe choice of payment methods\\nAdding a variety of payment methods makes things more convenient for users. It is recommended that you include:\\n\\n- Credit and debit cards (Visa, Mastercard, Mir)\\n- Electronic wallets (PayPal, WebMoney, Qiwi)\\n- Mobile payments (Apple Pay, Google Pay, Samsung Pay)\\n- Local methods (SBP, Alipay, iDEAL — depending on the region)\\nAn example of the code: a simple payment form with validation\\n\\nAdditional UX improvements\\n\\n- Autofill on the fields (autocomplete=\\"cc-number\\")\\n- Real-time validation with errors highlighted\\n- Security indicators (a padlock icon, an SSL certificate)\\n- The ability to save a card for future purchases\\n- Minimizing the number of steps (single-page payment)\\nConclusion: improving the UX of a payment form directly affects conversion and customer satisfaction. It is recommended that you test different variants and analyse user behaviour.","<form id=\\"payment-form\\">\\n  <label for=\\"card-number\\">Card number</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"card-number\\" placeholder=\\"1234 5678 9012 3456\\" required>\\n  <label for=\\"expiry\\">Expiry date</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"expiry\\" placeholder=\\"MM/YY\\" required>\\n  <label for=\\"cvv\\">CVV</label>\\n  <input type=\\"text\\" id=\\"cvv\\" placeholder=\\"123\\" required>\\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Pay</button>\\n</form>"],[502,"Pixel Perfect is an approach in which markup matches the design mock-up exactly. The main tools: browser extensions such as PerfectPixel (Chrome) and PixelPerfect (Firefox), together with the built-in developer tools. They let you overlay the mock-up on top of the page and compare the pixels.","What is Pixel Perfect and what is it for?\\nPixel Perfect is a layout technique in which the final HTML/CSS code reproduces the design mock-up (usually in PSD, Figma or Sketch format) as precisely as possible. This matters in order to ensure a uniform appearance on every device and in every browser, and also to meet the requirements of the client or the designer.\\nThe main tools\\n\\n- PerfectPixel (Chrome) — an extension that lets you upload an image of the mock-up and overlay it on top of the page with adjustable transparency.\\n- PixelPerfect (Firefox) — the equivalent for Firefox, with similar functionality.\\n- The built-in developer tools — in Chrome DevTools you can use the \\"Rendering\\" panel and the \\"Screenshot\\" option for comparison.\\n- Figma Mirror — for viewing the mock-up on mobile devices in real time.\\nAn example of using PerfectPixel\\n\\nPractical advice\\n\\n- Always check your markup at several screen resolutions.\\n- Use CSS properties such as `box-sizing: border-box` in order to avoid discrepancies in sizes.\\n- For complex mock-ups use grids (CSS Grid or Flexbox) for precise positioning.\\nConclusion: Pixel Perfect tools are essential for frontend developers working in a team with designers, in order to minimize the discrepancies between the mock-up and the finished product. They are especially useful at the layout stage and when work is being signed off.","// Install the PerfectPixel extension from the Chrome Web Store\\n// Open the page and click the extension\'s icon\\n// Upload an image of the mock-up (design.png, for example)\\n// Adjust the transparency (opacity) in order to compare\\n// Use the arrow keys for precise positioning"],[503,"Pixel Perfect markup is an approach in which the finished HTML/CSS code matches the design mock-up completely, down to the pixel. The developer uses tools for overlaying the markup on the mock-up and achieves a perfect match of sizes, spacing, colours and fonts. This calls for care and for skill with CSS.","What is Pixel Perfect markup?\\nPixel Perfect markup is a method of developing web interfaces in which the final HTML/CSS code is visually identical to the design mock-up supplied by the designer. The main aim is to make every element (blocks, text, images) match down to a single pixel. This approach is often used in projects where a high precision of visual presentation matters — in landing pages or corporate sites, for example.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe developer opens the mock-up (usually in Figma, Sketch or Photoshop) and lays the page out in the browser alongside it. For checking, special tools are used, such as browser extensions (PixelPerfect, for example) or plugins that overlay a semi-transparent image of the mock-up on top of the markup. The developer then adjusts the CSS properties (width, height, margin, padding, font-size, color and so on) until everything matches.\\nAn example of the code\\nSuppose the mock-up has a button 200x50 pixels, with a top margin of 20px and the font Arial 16px. The markup will look like this:\\n\\nAfter that the developer checks whether the button matches the mock-up and corrects the values if necessary.\\nWhen should it be used?\\nPixel Perfect markup is justified in projects with strict design requirements, where even a small deviation can be critical (branded sites, for example). In modern responsive and fluid layouts, however, this approach can be excessive, since the priority there is flexibility and ease of maintenance. Conclusion: use Pixel Perfect when precision matters more than development speed, but remember the trade-offs against responsiveness.","<button class=\\"btn\\">Click me</button>\\n\\n.btn {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n  margin-top: 20px;\\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\\n  font-size: 16px;\\n  background-color: #007bff;\\n  color: #fff;\\n  border: none;\\n  border-radius: 4px;\\n}"],[504,"The browser loads the HTML and the CSS and parses them into the DOM and the CSSOM. It then combines them into the render tree, computes the geometry of the elements (layout) and paints the pixels on the screen (paint). This process is called the critical rendering path.","How the browser processes CSS\\nWhen the browser loads a web page, it processes the HTML and the CSS in sequence. First the HTML is parsed, creating the DOM (Document Object Model). In parallel the CSS files are loaded and parsed, forming the CSSOM (CSS Object Model). These two trees are combined into the render tree, which contains only the visible elements.\\nThe rendering stages\\n\\n- Parsing the CSS: the browser analyses the CSS rules, resolves the conflicts (the cascade and specificity) and builds the CSSOM.\\n- Layout (Reflow): the size and the position of every element on the page are computed.\\n- Paint: the browser draws the pixels, filling in the colours, the shadows, the borders and so on.\\n- Composite: the layers are combined into the final image.\\nAn example\\n\\nThe browser will create a CSSOM node for .box, then compute its dimensions in layout, and fill it red in paint.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding this process helps you optimize performance: avoid frequent reflows and repaints, and use properties that do not cause a re-layout (transform, for example, instead of changing top/left).","/* CSS */\\n.box {\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 100px;\\n  background: red;\\n}"],[505,"A hidden element remains interactive if the property visibility: hidden or opacity: 0 is used. In the first case the element is invisible but takes up room and responds to clicks. In the second the element is completely transparent but also remains clickable. With display: none the element is removed from the flow and loses its interactivity.","How hiding affects interactivity\\nCSS has several ways of hiding an element, and their behaviour differs. If you want the element to remain interactive (to respond to clicks or focus, for example), you need to use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0`. With `display: none` the element is removed from the document flow entirely and loses all interactivity.\\nExamples of the code\\n\\nIn this example the element is invisible, but a click on its place will fire the handler. `opacity: 0` works in the same way — the element is transparent but remains in the flow and responds to events.\\nWhen this is useful\\n\\n- For creating appearance/disappearance animations without losing state.\\n- For hiding elements that must remain available to screen readers (with care).\\n- For implementing custom checkboxes or buttons, where a visually hidden element controls the behaviour.\\nConclusion: use `visibility: hidden` or `opacity: 0` if you need to keep a hidden element interactive — for animations or for accessibility, for example. To remove it from the interface entirely, apply `display: none`.","<div id=\\"hidden\\" style=\\"visibility: hidden;\\">Click me</div>\\n<script>\\n  document.getElementById(\'hidden\').addEventListener(\'click\', () => {\\n    alert(\'The click fired!\');\\n  });\\n<\/script>"],[506,"The display: none property removes an element from the document flow entirely; it takes up no room and is invisible. The visibility: hidden property makes an element invisible, but it goes on taking up room in the flow. Neither property removes the element from the DOM tree, but display: none excludes it from rendering.","The effect on the DOM and the document flow\\nThe properties `display: none` and `visibility: hidden` affect an element\'s display and its place in the document differently. Neither removes the element from the DOM tree, but they change how it takes part in rendering.\\ndisplay: none\\nThe element is removed from the document flow entirely. It takes up no room, and the neighbouring elements occupy its space. The element is invisible and takes no part in mouse or keyboard events.\\n\\nIn this example the second element is not displayed and takes up no room; the third element will come straight after the first.\\nvisibility: hidden\\nThe element becomes invisible but goes on taking up room in the document flow. It is not displayed, but its dimensions and its position are preserved, which affects the placement of the other elements.\\n\\nHere the second element is not visible, but between the first and the third there remains an empty space equal to its size.\\nUses\\n\\n- `display: none` is used to hide an element completely, when you need to free up the room (in responsive design, for example).\\n- `visibility: hidden` is used when you need to hide an element but keep its place (for animations or a placeholder, for example).\\nConclusion: the choice between these properties depends on the task — on whether the element\'s place in the document flow needs to be kept or whether it should be removed from the visual presentation entirely.","<div>The first element</div>\\n<div style=\\"display: none\\">The hidden element</div>\\n<div>The third element</div>\\n\\n<div>The first element</div>\\n<div style=\\"visibility: hidden\\">The invisible element</div>\\n<div>The third element</div>"],[507,"The pointer-events: none property disables every mouse and touch event for an element. The element becomes inactive for clicks, hovers and other interactions. This is useful for temporarily blocking buttons or for overlays.","How to disable interaction with an element through CSS\\nThe `pointer-events` property in CSS controls how an element responds to pointer events (mouse, touch, stylus). The value `none` disables every interaction completely: clicks, hovers, dragging and so on. The element becomes \\"transparent\\" to events, and they are passed through to the elements underneath.\\nAn example of its use\\n\\nIn this example the button does not respond to clicks, while the semi-transparency hints to the user that it is disabled.\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Temporarily blocking a form\'s submit button after it has been pressed\\n- Creating modal windows with a dimmed background (the overlay must not intercept clicks)\\n- Disabling links or navigation elements in particular states\\n- Implementing custom tooltips or dropdowns that must not get in the way of interacting with other elements\\nImportant subtleties\\n\\n- `pointer-events: none` does not affect keyboard navigation (Tab, Enter) — the element remains focusable. To disable it completely, use the `disabled` attribute or `tabindex=\\"-1\\"`.\\n- The property is inherited: if you apply it to a parent, every child element will lose its interactivity too.\\n- It is supported by every modern browser, including the mobile ones.\\nConclusion: `pointer-events: none` is a simple, effective way of temporarily disabling interaction with an element without changing the markup or the JavaScript logic. It is especially useful for loading states, modal windows and deactivating interface elements.","<button class=\\"disabled\\">Click me</button>\\n\\n.disabled {\\n  pointer-events: none;\\n  opacity: 0.5; /* we show visually that the button is inactive */\\n}"],[508,"Hidden elements are generally not indexed by search engines if they are hidden completely by means of CSS (display: none). If an element is hidden but its content is still available to be read (through visibility: hidden, for example), however, the search crawlers may index it. Using hidden elements to manipulate SEO can lead to penalties.","The effect of hidden elements on SEO\\nHidden elements on a web page can affect SEO differently depending on how they are hidden. Search engines such as Google aim to index the content that is visible to users, and may ignore hidden content or handle it differently.\\nThe main ways of hiding elements\\n\\n- display: none — the element is removed from the document flow entirely and is not displayed. Search crawlers normally do not index such content, since it is considered invisible to users.\\n- visibility: hidden — the element stays in the flow but becomes invisible. Its content may be indexed, since it is still present in the DOM.\\n- opacity: 0 — the element is transparent but takes up room. Search engines may index its content, since it is available to be read.\\n- position: absolute; left: -9999px — the element is shifted off the screen. This method is often used to hide text, but it may be judged an attempt to manipulate SEO.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nRecommendations\\nUse hidden elements only to improve the user experience — for modal windows or dropdown menus, for example. Do not try to hide keywords or links in order to manipulate your ranking, since that can lead to penalties from the search engines.\\nConclusion: hidden elements should be used with care, since their effect on SEO depends on the method of hiding. It is best to avoid hiding important content and to concentrate on creating good, visible content for users.","<!-- A hidden element with display: none -->\\n<div style=\\"display: none;\\">This text will not be indexed</div>\\n\\n<!-- A hidden element with visibility: hidden -->\\n<div style=\\"visibility: hidden;\\">This text may be indexed</div>\\n\\n<!-- A hidden element with opacity: 0 -->\\n<div style=\\"opacity: 0;\\">This text may also be indexed</div>"],[509,"The pointer-events: none property disables the handling of mouse and touch events for an element. That means the element will not respond to clicks, hovers or other pointer actions. It is often used to disable buttons temporarily, to create overlays, or to make an element \\"transparent\\" so that the elements underneath can be interacted with.","What is pointer-events: none?\\nThe `pointer-events` property in CSS controls how an element responds to pointer events (mouse, touchpad, stylus). The value `none` makes the element \\"invisible\\" to those events. This means that clicks, hovers, dragging and other pointer-initiated actions will pass straight through the element and be handled by whatever element lies beneath it (according to the stacking order).\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen a click happens on an element with `pointer-events: none`, the browser does not generate an event for that element. Instead the event is passed to the next element under the cursor, which may handle it. This property does not affect the element\'s visual display — it remains visible, but it stops being interactive.\\nExamples of its use\\n\\n- Disabling buttons temporarily: after a form has been submitted you can disable the button in order to prevent a second submission.\\n- Overlays and modal windows: if you have a semi-transparent overlay that must not block clicks on the elements beneath it, you can apply `pointer-events: none` to the overlay.\\n- Creating \\"clickable\\" areas: you can place an icon on top of a link, for example, where the click has to be handled by the link rather than by the icon.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nThe `pointer-events: none` property is a powerful tool for controlling the interactivity of elements without changing their visual presentation. It is worth applying when you need to disable the handling of pointer events for particular elements, temporarily or permanently — for creating non-clickable overlays, disabling buttons or redirecting events to the elements underneath, for example.","<style>\\n  .disabled-button {\\n    pointer-events: none;\\n    opacity: 0.5;\\n  }\\n  .overlay {\\n    position: absolute;\\n    top: 0;\\n    left: 0;\\n    width: 100%;\\n    height: 100%;\\n    background: rgba(0,0,0,0.3);\\n    pointer-events: none; /* Clicks pass through the overlay */\\n  }\\n</style>\\n\\n<button class=\\"disabled-button\\">Sent</button>\\n<div class=\\"overlay\\"></div>"],[510,"Hidden elements can be perceived differently by screen readers. Using the CSS property `display: none` or the `hidden` attribute removes an element from the accessibility tree entirely, and the screen reader ignores it. The `visibility: hidden` property also hides the element both visually and from the screen reader. If an element is hidden by means of `opacity: 0` or `clip`, however, it can remain available to the screen reader, which can confuse the user. For semantic hiding from screen readers, the attribute `aria-hidden=\\"true\\"` is used.","The effect of hidden elements on screen readers\\nScreen readers rely on the accessibility tree that the browser builds from the HTML code. The way an element is hidden directly affects whether it is included in that tree and therefore read out to the user.\\nThe main ways of hiding and their effect\\n\\n- display: none and the hidden attribute: they remove the element from the accessibility tree entirely. The screen reader does not see it and does not read it out. This is the correct way of hiding content that should not be available to anyone.\\n- visibility: hidden: similarly, the element is hidden both from the visual presentation and from the accessibility tree.\\n- opacity: 0 and clip: the element is visually invisible but remains in the accessibility tree. The screen reader can read it, which can disorient the user, since they cannot see the element on the screen.\\n- aria-hidden=\\"true\\": this attribute explicitly tells the screen reader to ignore the element, regardless of its visual state. It does not remove the element from the DOM but excludes it from the accessibility tree.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nTo ensure accessibility it is important to choose the right way of hiding elements. Use `display: none` or `hidden` for content that should not be available to anyone. Apply `aria-hidden=\\"true\\"` to decorative elements, or to content that is visually present but carries no meaning for screen-reader users. Avoid using `opacity: 0` to hide content that should not be read out.","<!-- The element is hidden from everyone -->\\n<div style=\\"display: none;\\">Nobody will see or hear this text</div>\\n\\n<!-- The element is visually hidden but available to a screen reader (bad practice) -->\\n<div style=\\"opacity: 0;\\">This text will be heard but not seen</div>\\n\\n<!-- The element is visually visible but hidden from the screen reader -->\\n<div aria-hidden=\\"true\\">This text will be seen but not heard</div>"],[511,"Elements with position: absolute and position: fixed fall out of the document flow, as do elements with float (other than none). They stop affecting the placement of the neighbouring elements and can overlap one another. This is used to create pop-ups, navigation bars and complex grids.","What the document flow is and how elements fall out of it\\nThe document flow is the natural order in which elements are displayed on a page, where each block element sits below the previous one and inline ones sit alongside. Some CSS properties let you take an element out of that flow, which changes the behaviour of the surrounding elements.\\nThe main ways of falling out of the flow\\n\\n- position: absolute — the element is positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor (or to the body), falling out of the flow entirely. The neighbouring elements take its place.\\n- position: fixed — the same as absolute, but it is tied to the browser window and stays where it is as the page scrolls.\\n- float: left/right — the element is shifted to the left or to the right, and the text and inline elements flow around it. The element itself falls out of the flow, but its content can still affect the height of its parent (a clearfix is needed).\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse falling out of the flow for precise positioning (modal windows, tooltips) or for making text flow around something (images in articles). Remember that it can disturb the usual layout, so apply it deliberately.","<div style=\\"position: relative; height: 100px;\\">\\n  <div style=\\"position: absolute; top: 0; left: 0;\\">Absolute</div>\\n  <div>An ordinary block</div>\\n</div>\\n<div style=\\"float: left; width: 50px;\\">Floating</div>\\n<p>The text flows around the floating element.</p>"],[512,"Gaps appear between inline-block elements because the browser interprets the spaces and line breaks in the HTML code as text nodes. Those spaces are displayed as ordinary spaces between the elements, creating visible gaps. To remove them you can use font-size: 0 on the parent, comments between the tags, or write the elements on a single line.","The reason the gaps appear\\nWhen you use display: inline-block on elements, the browser treats them as inline blocks. In HTML the spaces, tabs and line breaks between tags count as text nodes. Since inline-block elements behave like text, those spaces are displayed as visible gaps.\\nAn example of the problem\\n\\nA gap about 4px wide will appear between the red and the blue squares.\\nThe ways of solving it\\n\\n- Remove the whitespace from the markup: write the elements on a single line with no spaces.\\n- Use comments: <!-- --> between the tags.\\n- font-size: 0: set font-size: 0 on the parent and then set the font size explicitly on the child elements.\\n- A negative margin: apply margin-right: -4px (it depends on the font size).\\nAn example with font-size: 0\\n\\nThis method is often used in practice, since it requires no change to the HTML structure.\\nConclusion\\nThe gaps between inline-block elements are a consequence of the way whitespace is handled in HTML. Understanding this helps you avoid unexpected gaps in your layout. It is recommended that you use font-size: 0 on the parent or write the elements on a single line for cleanliness of code.","<div>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red;\\"></span>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue;\\"></span>\\n</div>\\n\\n<div style=\\"font-size: 0;\\">\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: red; font-size: 16px;\\"></span>\\n  <span style=\\"display: inline-block; width: 50px; height: 50px; background: blue; font-size: 16px;\\"></span>\\n</div>"],[513,"Inline styles are styles set directly in an HTML element\'s style attribute. They have a high priority, but their use is justified only in rare cases — for dynamic styles that depend on JavaScript, for example, or for quick prototyping. In production it is better to use CSS classes, since they improve readability, reusability and performance.","What inline styles are and when they are used\\nInline styles are a way of setting CSS properties directly in an HTML element\'s `style` attribute. For example: `<div style=\\"color: red; font-size: 16px;\\">Text</div>`. This approach lets you apply styles to a particular element quickly without creating separate CSS rules. Its use has both advantages and disadvantages, however.\\nThe pros and cons of inline styles\\n\\n- The pros: a high priority (it overrides most other styles), convenience for dynamic styles (when changing them through JavaScript, for example), simplicity for quick testing.\\n- The cons: poor reusability (the styles are tied to a particular element), difficulty of maintenance (a change requires editing every element), growth in the size of the HTML, the impossibility of using pseudo-classes and media queries, reduced performance when they are changed frequently through the DOM.\\nExamples of their use\\nHere is an example where inline styles may be justified — changing a colour dynamically according to state:\\n\\nFor static styling, however, it is better to use classes:\\n\\nConclusion\\nInline styles are worth applying only for dynamic changes that cannot be implemented through CSS classes, or in extremely small projects. In most cases external CSS files or CSS modules are preferable, for better maintainability and performance.","const button = document.getElementById(\'myButton\');\\nbutton.style.backgroundColor = isActive ? \'green\' : \'red\';\\n\\n<style>\\n  .active { background-color: green; }\\n  .inactive { background-color: red; }\\n</style>\\n<div class=\\"active\\">A button</div>"],[514,"On the desktop the browser most often used is Google Chrome. Its market share is around 65–70%. The other popular browsers are Safari, Firefox and Edge. The statistics can change over time.","Which browser is most often used on the desktop?\\nOn desktop computers the most popular browser is Google Chrome. According to various analytics services, its market share is consistently around 65–70%. That means roughly two out of every three PC users open web pages in Chrome.\\nWhy does Chrome lead?\\n\\n- Speed and performance: Chrome is known for loading pages quickly and for working with JavaScript efficiently.\\n- Extensions: the enormous library of extensions in the Chrome Web Store lets you configure the browser for any task.\\n- Integration with Google\'s services: convenient synchronization with Gmail, Google Drive, YouTube and other services.\\n- Security: regular updates and built-in mechanisms for protection against malicious sites.\\nThe other popular browsers\\n\\n- Safari: in second place; it is especially popular among macOS users.\\n- Firefox: valued for its privacy and its open source code.\\n- Microsoft Edge: gaining popularity thanks to its integration with Windows and its use of the Chromium engine.\\nConclusion\\nWhen developing web applications it is important to test them in Google Chrome first of all, since that will ensure they work correctly for the majority of users. The other browsers should not be forgotten, however, in order to guarantee compatibility across different platforms.",null],[515,"Yes, older device models and browsers need to be taken into account if your target audience uses them. That ensures the application is accessible and works correctly for every user. Apply progressive enhancement or graceful degradation, so that the basic functionality works everywhere while the modern capabilities are added for newer browsers.","Why take older devices into account?\\nSupport for older device models and browsers matters in order to keep your web application accessible. If a significant part of your target audience uses obsolete browsers (Internet Explorer 11 or old versions of Safari on iOS, for example), ignoring them can lead to a loss of users and a drop in conversion.\\nThe approaches to supporting them\\n\\n- Progressive enhancement — first a basic version is built that works on every device, and then modern features are added for newer browsers.\\n- Graceful degradation — first the full version is developed for modern browsers, and then correct operation (possibly with a simplified interface) is ensured on older ones.\\nAn example of the code: checking for CSS Grid support\\n\\nIn this example modern browsers use Grid while older ones use Flexbox as the fallback.\\nConclusion\\nTaking older devices into account is necessary if your audience uses them actively. Apply progressive enhancement in order to ensure the basic functionality on every platform, and add the modern capabilities as an enhancement for newer browsers.","@supports (display: grid) {\\n  .container {\\n    display: grid;\\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/* The fallback for older browsers */\\n.container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  flex-wrap: wrap;\\n}\\n\\n.container > * {\\n  flex: 1 1 50%;\\n}"],[516,"Open the developer tools (F12), go to the Elements tab and select the element. In the Styles panel find the CSS property color or background-color. Click the little coloured square next to the value in order to open the palette and see the exact colour.","Checking a colour with the browser\'s tools\\nThe browser\'s developer tools provide a convenient way of inspecting the colours used on a web page. This is necessary for precise layout, for debugging styles and for making sure things match the design.\\nThe main steps\\n\\n- Open the developer tools (F12 or Ctrl+Shift+I).\\n- Go to the Elements tab (or Inspector).\\n- Select the element with the picker tool (the cursor icon) or click on the HTML code.\\n- In the right-hand panel find the Styles tab (or Computed).\\n- Find the CSS property that sets the colour (color, background-color, border-color, for example).\\n- Next to the colour value (#ff0000, for example) there will be a little coloured square — click it.\\nWorking with the palette\\nAfter the click a colour palette will open, where you can:\\n\\n- See the exact colour value in HEX, RGB, HSL or other formats.\\n- Change the colour by dragging the sliders or entering values.\\n- Copy the colour value to the clipboard.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn the developer tools you will see these colours and will be able to check them.\\nConclusion\\nUse the developer tools to check and correct colours quickly in real time, which is especially useful when laying things out and debugging styles.","<div style=\\"color: #3498db; background-color: #f1c40f;\\">Some example text</div>"],[517,"A HEX colour is a hexadecimal representation of a colour, used in web design. It consists of a hash symbol (#) and six hexadecimal digits that determine the intensity of the red, green and blue channels. #FF0000, for example, means red. HEX colours are widely used in CSS to set the colours of elements.","What is a HEX colour?\\nA HEX colour is a way of representing a colour in hexadecimal, widely used in web development and design. It is based on the RGB model, where each colour is set by three channels: red, green and blue. Each channel is encoded by two hexadecimal digits from 00 to FF, which corresponds to the decimal values from 0 to 255.\\nThe structure of a HEX colour\\nA HEX colour begins with a hash symbol (#), followed by six characters: the first two are the red channel, the next two the green, and the last two the blue. #FF0000, for example, means the maximum intensity of red (255) and no green or blue, that is, pure red.\\nExamples and uses\\nHEX colours are used in CSS to set the colours of backgrounds, text, borders and other elements. Here is an example of the code:\\n\\nThere are also shortened three-character HEX codes — #F00, for example, is equivalent to #FF0000. That is convenient for simple colours.\\nConclusion\\nHEX colours are the standard, convenient way of setting colours in web design, especially in CSS. They give precise control over colour and are easy to read, which makes them indispensable to interface developers.","/* A red background */\\nbackground-color: #FF0000;\\n\\n/* Green text */\\ncolor: #00FF00;\\n\\n/* A blue border */\\nborder: 2px solid #0000FF;"],[518,"Spacing in an interface is set by means of the CSS property margin. It creates room around an element, separating it from its neighbours. The values can be given in pixels, percentages or other units. The margin property affects an element\'s outer area within the box model.","What margin is and how it works\\nThe margin property in CSS determines an element\'s outer spacing — the room between its border and the neighbouring elements. It is part of the box model, which comprises content, padding, border and margin. A margin has no background and is always transparent.\\nThe ways of setting margin\\n\\n- One value: `margin: 10px;` — the spacing is the same on every side.\\n- Two values: `margin: 10px 20px;` — the first for the top/bottom, the second for the left/right.\\n- Four values: `margin: 10px 15px 20px 25px;` — clockwise: top, right, bottom, left.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe peculiarities of margin\\n\\n- Margin collapsing: the vertical spacing of adjacent block elements is merged into a single one equal to the larger of them.\\n- Negative values: they let you overlap elements or reduce the distance between them.\\n- Automatic spacing: `margin: 0 auto;` centres a block element horizontally.\\nConclusion\\nMargin is the basic tool for controlling the distance between elements in a web interface. It is worth using to create visual separation between blocks, to centre things and to fine-tune a page\'s composition.","<div class=\\"box\\">Element 1</div>\\n<div class=\\"box\\">Element 2</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n.box {\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n  background: lightblue;\\n  margin: 20px; /* spacing on every side */\\n}\\n</style>"],[519,"On iOS every browser, Chrome and Firefox included, is obliged to use Apple\'s WebKit engine. That means the default and the only fully fledged browser is Safari, since the others are merely shells over it. The user can install alternatives, but they have no rendering engines of their own.","Which browser is used by default on iOS?\\nOn iOS devices (iPhone, iPad) the default browser is Safari. It is a preinstalled application from Apple that uses the WebKit engine. It is important to understand that, because of Apple\'s policy, every third-party browser (Chrome, Firefox, Edge, for example) is also obliged to use WebKit rather than its own engine (Blink or Gecko). Technically, therefore, every browser on iOS is a shell over Safari with a different interface and different synchronization.\\nWhy does this matter?\\nThis restriction affects web development: if you test a site in Chrome on iOS, it is rendered exactly as it is in Safari. To check compatibility, therefore, it is enough to go by Safari and WebKit. The user can change the default browser in the settings, but that will not change the engine.\\nAn example of code for detecting the browser on iOS\\n\\nConclusion\\nKnowing this peculiarity helps you avoid mistakes in cross-browser testing and in optimizing for Apple\'s mobile devices. Always check sites in Safari on iOS, since it is the only browser with full access to the platform\'s features.","// Checking whether the browser is Safari on iOS\\nconst isIOS = /iPad|iPhone|iPod/.test(navigator.userAgent);\\nconst isSafari = /^((?!chrome|android).)*safari/i.test(navigator.userAgent);\\nif (isIOS && isSafari) {\\n  console.log(\'This is Safari on iOS\');\\n} else if (isIOS) {\\n  console.log(\'Another browser on iOS (but it uses WebKit)\');\\n}"],[520,"Browsers can run on the same engine. Google Chrome, Opera, Brave, Microsoft Edge (from 2020 onwards) and Vivaldi, for example, use the Blink engine (a fork of WebKit). Safari uses WebKit. Firefox uses Gecko. This matters for cross-browser testing.","What is a browser engine?\\nA browser engine (rendering engine) is the browser\'s key component, responsible for parsing HTML and CSS and displaying a web page on the screen. Different browsers can use one and the same engine, which means similar behaviour when rendering, though it does not guarantee complete identity because of the differences in their other components (the JavaScript engine, the security settings and so on).\\nThe main engines and the browsers based on them\\n\\n- Blink (Google\'s fork of WebKit): Google Chrome, Microsoft Edge (Chromium-based), Opera, Brave, Vivaldi, Samsung Internet.\\n- WebKit: Apple Safari, and every browser on iOS (because of Apple\'s requirements).\\n- Gecko: Mozilla Firefox, Tor Browser.\\n- Goanna (a fork of Gecko): Pale Moon, Basilisk.\\nAn example of code for checking the engine\\n\\nWhy does this matter?\\nKnowing the engine helps you predict how a page will be displayed in different browsers. CSS properties supported in Blink, for example, may not work in Gecko. This is critical when developing cross-browser applications.\\nConclusion: understanding which engines are shared simplifies the testing and optimization of web applications, letting you concentrate on the key differences between the main engines (Blink, WebKit, Gecko).","// Detecting the browser engine through navigator.userAgent\\nfunction getEngine() {\\n  const ua = navigator.userAgent;\\n  if (ua.includes(\'Gecko\') && !ua.includes(\'WebKit\')) return \'Gecko\';\\n  if (ua.includes(\'AppleWebKit\') && !ua.includes(\'Chrome\')) return \'WebKit\';\\n  if (ua.includes(\'AppleWebKit\') && ua.includes(\'Chrome\')) return \'Blink\';\\n  return \'Unknown\';\\n}\\nconsole.log(getEngine());"],[521,"The viewport is the visible area of a web page on a device\'s screen. Without configuring it properly, mobile browsers may scale the page, making it awkward to use. To control it, the `<meta name=\\"viewport\\">` tag is used. It is the foundation of responsive layout.","What is the viewport?\\nThe viewport is the rectangular area of the screen in which a web page is displayed. On a desktop it coincides with the browser window; on mobile devices it can be smaller than the physical screen. Without an explicit viewport declaration, browsers on phones often try to show the whole page at once, reducing its scale, which makes the text and the elements too small.\\nWhy control the viewport?\\nTo build responsive sites that display correctly on any device, the viewport\'s width has to be set equal to the width of the device\'s screen. This is achieved by means of a meta tag in the HTML. If you do not do it, users will have to scale the page by hand, which makes the experience worse.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe viewport meta tag tells the browser how to control the page\'s dimensions and scaling. The most common form is:\\n\\nThe `width=device-width` parameter sets the viewport\'s width equal to the width of the device\'s screen. `initial-scale=1.0` sets the initial scale to 100% (no zoom).\\nAn example of its use\\nConsider a simple page without the viewport tag and with it:\\n\\nWithout the tag the text will be small on a mobile and the user will have to zoom in. With the tag the text is displayed at its natural size.\\nConclusion\\nThe viewport is a critically important concept for responsive web design. Configuring it properly is essential for any site aimed at mobile devices, and it is the first step towards building a convenient user interface.","<meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n\\n<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <!-- Without this tag a mobile browser will shrink the page -->\\n  <meta name=\\"viewport\\" content=\\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\\">\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <p>Text that will be readable on a phone.</p>\\n</body>\\n</html>"],[522,"Deviations from the mock-up are recorded by means of visual comparison: a screenshot of the markup is overlaid on the mock-up in an overlay mode (in the Pixel Perfect plugin, for example). Visual regression testing tools (Percy, Applitools) are also used, which compare screenshots automatically. It is important to document every discrepancy in the bug tracker, with a screenshot and a description.","How to record deviations from the mock-up\\nDeviations from the mock-up are discrepancies between the implemented interface and the approved design. Recording them is critical to maintaining the quality of the product and the uniformity of the user experience. The main aim is to spot the discrepancies at the early stages of development, in order to avoid an accumulation of technical debt.\\nThe methods of recording\\n\\n- Manual visual comparison: use browser extensions (Pixel Perfect, PerfectPixel) to overlay a screenshot of the mock-up on top of the markup. Adjust the transparency and check the spacing, the font sizes and the colours.\\n- Automated visual regression testing: tools (Percy, Applitools, Chromatic) take screenshots of the components and compare them against reference ones. If there is a discrepancy, a report is generated with the changes highlighted.\\n- Inspecting the code: use DevTools to check the CSS properties (padding, margin, font-size, color) and compare them against the values from the mock-up (Figma, Sketch).\\nAn example of code for an automated check\\n\\nThe documentation process\\n\\n- Take a screenshot of the problem area.\\n- State the exact location (the URL, the component, the screen resolution).\\n- Describe the expected behaviour (from the mock-up) and the actual one.\\n- Attach a link to the mock-up in Figma or another tool.\\n- Create a task in the bug tracker (Jira, Trello) with a priority.\\nConclusion: recording deviations from the mock-up is an essential part of the QA process. Manual methods are suited to spot checks, while automated tools save time in regression testing. Use a combination of approaches for maximum coverage.","// An example of using Percy for visual testing\\nimport PercyScript from \'@percy/script\';\\n\\nPercyScript.run(async (page, percySnapshot) => {\\n  await page.goto(\'http://localhost:3000\');\\n  // We take a snapshot of the page\\n  await percySnapshot(\'Homepage\');\\n  // If there are discrepancies, Percy will flag them in the report\\n});"],[523,"There are several ways of centring elements in CSS. For centring block elements horizontally you can use margin: 0 auto. To centre text or inline elements, text-align: center is applied. With Flexbox you can easily centre elements both horizontally and vertically, using justify-content: center and align-items: center. CSS Grid also offers centring through place-items: center.","The main ways of centring in CSS\\nCentring elements is one of the most frequent tasks in web layout. CSS has several approaches, each suited to a different situation. Let us look at the main methods.\\nHorizontal centring\\nFor block elements with a set width you can use the `margin: 0 auto` property. This works when the element has a fixed width and is a block element.\\n\\nFor inline elements (text or images, for example) `text-align: center` is used on the parent container.\\n\\nVertical centring\\nVertical centring used to call for complicated tricks with `position: absolute` and negative offsets. Now Flexbox or Grid are preferable.\\nFlexbox\\nFlexbox offers a simple way of centring both horizontally and vertically.\\n\\nThis method works for any number of child elements and does not require you to state any dimensions.\\nCSS Grid\\nGrid also lets you centre elements easily.\\n\\nThe `place-items` property is shorthand for `align-items` and `justify-items`.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of centring method depends on the context. For simple layouts `margin: auto` or `text-align` will do. For modern responsive interfaces it is better to use Flexbox or Grid, since they give flexibility and are easy to maintain.",".block {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  margin: 0 auto;\\n}\\n\\n.parent {\\n  text-align: center;\\n}\\n\\n.parent {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: center;\\n  align-items: center;\\n  height: 100vh;\\n}\\n\\n.parent {\\n  display: grid;\\n  place-items: center;\\n  height: 100vh;\\n}"],[524,"An absolutely positioned element is positioned relative to its nearest ancestor whose positioning is other than static. If there is no such ancestor, then relative to the initial containing block (usually the browser window). This lets you place elements precisely inside a parent container.","How absolute positioning works\\nAn absolutely positioned element (position: absolute) is removed from the normal document flow entirely and positioned relative to its nearest positioned ancestor. An ancestor counts as positioned if its position property is set to relative, absolute, fixed or sticky (any value other than static).\\nFinding the positioning context\\nIf the element has no ancestor with explicit positioning, it will be positioned relative to the initial containing block. In web browsers that block is usually the viewport, but formally it is the root html element.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the inner div with position: absolute will sit 20px from the top and 30px from the left of the outer div\'s bounds, since the outer div has position: relative.\\nConclusion\\nAbsolute positioning is convenient for creating tooltips, modal windows, dropdown menus and other elements that have to be placed precisely relative to a parent container without affecting the placement of the neighbouring elements.","<div style=\\"position: relative; width: 300px; height: 200px;\\">\\n  <div style=\\"position: absolute; top: 20px; left: 30px;\\">\\n    I am positioned relative to my parent\\n  </div>\\n</div>"],[525,"Responsive layout is an approach in which a site\'s design adjusts to the size of the device\'s screen. Media queries (CSS media queries) are used in order to change the styles according to the width of the browser window. This lets a site look equally good on desktops, tablets and smartphones. The main aim is to keep reading and navigation comfortable without horizontal scrolling.","What is responsive layout?\\nResponsive layout (responsive web design) is a method of creating web pages that adjust automatically to the size of the user\'s device screen. Unlike fixed layout, where the width is set in pixels, responsive layout uses relative units (percentages, em, rem) and media queries in order to change the layout.\\nThe main principles\\n\\n- A flexible grid: the elements are laid out as percentages of the width of their parent container.\\n- Flexible images: pictures scale by means of max-width: 100%.\\n- Media queries: CSS rules that are applied under particular conditions (when the screen is narrower than 768px, for example).\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nResponsive layout is used on most modern sites, especially in online shops, blogs and news portals. It is critically important for SEO, since Google gives preference to mobile-friendly sites.\\nConclusion: responsive layout is the standard of modern web design. It is worth applying whenever a site has to be available on different devices, in order to ensure a good user experience and high positions in the search results.","/* The basic grid */\\n.container {\\n  width: 100%;\\n  max-width: 1200px;\\n  margin: 0 auto;\\n}\\n\\n/* A media query for tablets */\\n@media (max-width: 768px) {\\n  .container {\\n    padding: 0 15px;\\n  }\\n  .sidebar {\\n    display: none;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n/* A media query for phones */\\n@media (max-width: 480px) {\\n  .header {\\n    font-size: 18px;\\n  }\\n}"],[526,"Flex-shrink determines how much a flex item will shrink if the combined width of the items exceeds the width of the container. The default value is 1, which lets the item shrink. If you set 0, the item will not shrink. The greater the value, the stronger the shrinking.","What is flex-shrink?\\nFlex-shrink is a CSS property that governs a flex item\'s ability to shrink in size when the items together do not fit in the flex container. It works in tandem with flex-grow (growing) and flex-basis (the base size). By default flex-shrink is 1, which means the item can shrink in proportion to its base size.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the sum of every item\'s base size (flex-basis) exceeds the space available in the container, the browser computes how much each item needs to be reduced by. The flex-shrink value acts as a multiplier: an item with flex-shrink: 2 will shrink twice as much as an item with flex-shrink: 1. If you set flex-shrink: 0, the item will not shrink at all, which can lead to the container overflowing.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the total width of the items is 600px while the container is 300px. The shortfall is 300px. The item .item3 does not shrink, so .item1 and .item2 divide the remaining overflow (300px) in proportion to their flex-shrink: .item1 will take 1/3 (100px) and .item2 2/3 (200px).\\nWhere is it used?\\nFlex-shrink is useful when building responsive layouts, where the items have to adjust to the size of the screen without spilling out past the container\'s bounds — in product cards or navigation bars, for example.\\nConclusion: use flex-shrink to control which items will shrink when room is short, and set different values for the behaviour you want to prioritize.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  width: 300px;\\n}\\n.item1 {\\n  flex-basis: 200px;\\n  flex-shrink: 1; /* it will shrink */\\n}\\n.item2 {\\n  flex-basis: 200px;\\n  flex-shrink: 2; /* it will shrink more */\\n}\\n.item3 {\\n  flex-basis: 200px;\\n  flex-shrink: 0; /* it will not shrink */\\n}"],[527,"Font loading can block the rendering of text, causing delays. Browsers use different strategies: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text) — the text is invisible until the font has loaded — or FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text) — the text is displayed in a fallback font. This affects the perceived loading speed and can make the user experience worse.","The effect of font loading on rendering\\nWhen a web page uses custom fonts, the browser has to load them before it can display the text. This process can delay rendering and affect performance. There are two main behaviours: FOIT (Flash of Invisible Text) and FOUT (Flash of Unstyled Text).\\nFOIT and FOUT\\n\\n- FOIT: the browser hides the text until the font has loaded. This can leave empty space on the page.\\n- FOUT: the browser displays the text in a fallback font first and replaces it with the custom one once it has loaded. This causes a visible change of style.\\nHow to optimize font loading\\nUse `font-display` in your CSS to control the behaviour. For example:\\n\\nThe value `swap` enables FOUT, which improves the perceived speed. You can also preload fonts by means of `<link rel=\\"preload\\">`.\\nConclusion\\nConfiguring font loading properly is critical to performance and to the UX. Use `font-display: swap` and preloading in order to minimize delays and avoid invisible text.","@font-face {\\n  font-family: \'MyFont\';\\n  src: url(\'myfont.woff2\') format(\'woff2\');\\n  font-display: swap; /* It uses FOUT */\\n}"],[528,"The repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...)) function creates as many tracks as fit in the container, with a minimum and a maximum size. auto-fill creates empty tracks if there are fewer items, while auto-fit collapses them, letting the items stretch. This is convenient for responsive grids without media queries.","How repeat(auto-fill, minmax(...)) works in CSS Grid\\nThe `repeat()` function in CSS Grid lets you set a repeating pattern of tracks. When the first argument is `auto-fill` or `auto-fit`, the browser computes the number of tracks that fit in the container automatically, based on the minimum size given through `minmax()`. This is the key mechanism for building responsive grids without media queries.\\nThe difference between auto-fill and auto-fit\\nBoth values work similarly, but they handle empty space differently. `auto-fill` creates as many tracks as fit, even if there are fewer items — the empty tracks remain visible (they take up room). `auto-fit` collapses the empty tracks, letting the existing items stretch into the space that is freed up.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn the first case, if there are fewer items than there are tracks that fit, empty columns will appear. In the second the empty columns will vanish and the items will take up the whole width.\\nWhere it is used\\nThis approach is widely used to create responsive cards, galleries and product lists — when the number of items is not known in advance and the grid has to adjust to the width of the screen.\\nConclusion: use `auto-fill` if you need to keep an even grid with a fixed number of columns, and `auto-fit` when the items should stretch to fill all of the available space.",".grid {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));\\n  gap: 10px;\\n}\\n\\n.grid-fit {\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(150px, 1fr));\\n}"],[529,"CSS variables (custom properties) can be changed dynamically through JavaScript, by setting their values through an element\'s style property or through setProperty. In CSS Modules the variables are declared in the global `:root` selector or locally. To change one dynamically it is enough to reach the DOM element and set a new value for the variable that the module\'s styles use.","How passing CSS variables through JavaScript into CSS Modules works\\nCSS variables (custom properties) let you hold values that can be reused across your styles. In CSS Modules the variables are declared just as they are in ordinary CSS, but with the module\'s local scope in mind. To change a component\'s style dynamically you need to set a new value for the CSS variable on the DOM element in question through JavaScript.\\nAn example with React\\nSuppose we have a component that uses a CSS Module with the variable --primary-color. We want to change the colour on a click.\\n\\nIn this example clicking the button calls changeColor, which uses setProperty to change the value of --primary-color to \'red\'. The button\'s style updates instantly, since a CSS variable is reactive.\\nWhere this is used\\n\\n- Themes (light/dark)\\n- Dynamic animations\\n- Responsive components that depend on state\\nConclusion: using CSS variables together with JavaScript is a simple, effective way of controlling styles dynamically without reloading the page or changing classes. It is especially useful in component frameworks, where the appearance has to change according to user interaction or data.","// styles.module.css\\n:root {\\n  --primary-color: blue;\\n}\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: var(--primary-color);\\n  color: white;\\n  padding: 10px 20px;\\n}\\n\\n// Button.jsx\\nimport React, { useRef } from \'react\';\\nimport styles from \'./styles.module.css\';\\n\\nfunction Button() {\\n  const buttonRef = useRef(null);\\n\\n  const changeColor = () => {\\n    if (buttonRef.current) {\\n      buttonRef.current.style.setProperty(\'--primary-color\', \'red\');\\n    }\\n  };\\n\\n  return (\\n    <button ref={buttonRef} className={styles.button} onClick={changeColor}>\\n      Click me\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nexport default Button;"],[530,"CSS modules solve the problem of class-name collisions by generating unique class names automatically at build time. Each class from a CSS file is turned into a hashed string that is guaranteed to be unique across the whole application. This lets developers use short, clear names without any risk of clashing with other components.","How CSS modules solve the problem of class-name collisions\\nCSS modules are an approach in which a component\'s styles are isolated at build time. The main problem they solve is CSS\'s global namespace, where two identical classes from different files can conflict. CSS modules generate unique class names automatically, adding a hash or another identifier to them, which rules collisions out.\\nThe principle\\nAt build time (with Webpack and css-loader, for example) each CSS file is processed as a module. Every class name in it is turned into a unique string, and in the JavaScript code they are imported as an object whose keys are the original names and whose values are the generated ones. This lets you use short names in development while guaranteeing uniqueness at run time.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- In React, Vue, Angular and other frameworks, to isolate components\' styles.\\n- In projects with a large codebase, where it matters to avoid accidental overrides.\\n- When using CSS preprocessors (Sass, Less) in combination with modules.\\nConclusion\\nCSS modules are worth applying in any modern web project that uses a component-based approach. They make styles predictable, make refactoring easier and remove the headache of global CSS conflicts, especially in teams.","// styles.module.css\\n.button {\\n  background: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n}\\n\\n// Component.jsx\\nimport styles from \'./styles.module.css\';\\n\\nfunction Button() {\\n  return <button className={styles.button}>Click me</button>;\\n}\\n\\n// In the browser the class will look like \\"_button_1a2b3\\""],[531,"CSS-in-JS is slower than native CSS because it adds runtime costs: parsing JavaScript, generating the styles and inserting them into the DOM while the application is running. Native CSS is processed by the browser directly, without any additional computation. This is especially noticeable with frequent re-renders or a great many dynamic styles.","Why is CSS-in-JS slower than native CSS?\\nCSS-in-JS is an approach in which styles are defined inside the JavaScript code, often by means of libraries such as styled-components or Emotion. Unlike native CSS, which the browser loads and applies immediately, CSS-in-JS requires additional steps at run time, which can slow rendering down.\\nThe main reasons for the slowdown\\n\\n- Runtime parsing and generation: every time a component renders, the CSS-in-JS library parses the template strings, generates unique class names and creates the CSS rules.\\n- Insertion into the DOM: the generated styles are inserted into a `<style>` element in the DOM, which triggers a style recalculation (recalc style) and can lead to a repaint.\\n- The absence of caching: unlike native CSS, which is cached by the browser, CSS-in-JS often generates new styles on every render, especially if dynamic values are used.\\nA comparison example\\nNative CSS:\\n\\nCSS-in-JS (styled-components):\\n\\nOn every render of `Button` with a different `color`, the library creates a new class and inserts it into the DOM, which takes time.\\nWhen is this critical?\\nPerformance problems become noticeable in large applications with frequent re-renders (animations, lists with thousands of items, for example). For static pages the difference may be insignificant.\\nConclusion\\nCSS-in-JS is convenient for dynamic styles and for isolation, but if performance is critical it is better to use native CSS or CSS Modules, which are processed at build time and carry no runtime overhead.",".button { color: blue; }\\n\\nconst Button = styled.button`\\n  color: ${props => props.color};\\n`;"],[532,"CSS-in-JS is an approach in which styles are written inside the JavaScript code, usually by means of libraries such as styled-components. The performance downside is that the styles are computed and injected into the DOM at run time, which can cause delays when rendering. CSS modules, by contrast, generate static CSS files at build time, which is faster.","What is CSS-in-JS?\\nCSS-in-JS is a methodology in which CSS styles are defined directly inside JavaScript files by means of special libraries such as styled-components, Emotion or JSS. This lets you use the full power of JavaScript to create dynamic styles — changing colours according to a component\'s props, for example.\\nHow does it work?\\nWith CSS-in-JS the styles are normally created as objects or template strings, which are then turned into real CSS rules and inserted into the DOM through a `<style>` tag while the application is running. For example:\\n\\nThis code creates a Button component whose styles are computed dynamically from its props.\\nThe performance downside compared with CSS modules\\nCSS-in-JS\'s main disadvantage is its runtime overhead. Every time a component renders, the library has to compute the styles, create the CSS classes and insert them into the DOM. This can cause additional delays, especially with a great many components or frequent re-renders. CSS modules, by contrast, generate static CSS files at build time. The styles are loaded once and require no computation in the browser, which makes them more performant.\\nA comparison example\\nCSS modules:\\n\\nHere the styles are already ready to use after the build, with no additional computation.\\nConclusion\\nCSS-in-JS is convenient for dynamic styles and for isolation, but it can reduce performance because of its runtime computation. CSS modules are preferable for static styles and for projects where loading and rendering speed matters.","import styled from \'styled-components\';\\n\\nconst Button = styled.button`\\n  background: ${props => props.primary ? \'blue\' : \'gray\'};\\n  color: white;\\n  padding: 10px;\\n`;\\n\\n/* Button.module.css */\\n.button {\\n  background: blue;\\n  color: white;\\n}\\n\\n// The React component\\nimport styles from \'./Button.module.css\';\\n\\nfunction Button() {\\n  return <button className={styles.button}>Click</button>;\\n}"],[533,"To add support for a CSS preprocessor — Sass, for example — you need to install the corresponding loaders: sass-loader, css-loader and style-loader. Then, in the Webpack configuration, add a rule for .scss files in the module.rules section, listing those loaders in right-to-left order. This will let Webpack process Sass files, turn them into CSS and inject them into the HTML.","How to add support for CSS preprocessors in Webpack\\nCSS preprocessors such as Sass, Less and Stylus extend the capabilities of ordinary CSS, adding variables, nesting, mixins and other features. For Webpack to be able to process those preprocessors\' files, the corresponding loaders have to be configured. Loaders are modules that transform files before they are added to the bundle.\\nInstalling the necessary packages\\nTo work with Sass (the most popular preprocessor) three packages are needed:\\n\\n- `sass-loader` — compiles Sass/SCSS into CSS.\\n- `css-loader` — interprets `@import` and `url()` in the CSS.\\n- `style-loader` — injects the CSS into the DOM through a `<style>` tag.\\nInstall them with the command:\\n\\nConfiguring Webpack\\nIn the `webpack.config.js` file we add a rule for files with the extension `.scss` or `.sass`:\\n\\nThe order of the loaders matters: Webpack applies them from right to left. First `sass-loader` turns the SCSS into CSS, then `css-loader` processes the CSS, and finally `style-loader` adds the styles to the HTML.\\nAn example of its use\\nOnce it is configured you can import SCSS files in your JavaScript:\\n\\nWebpack will process the file and add the styles to the page automatically.\\nConclusion\\nConfiguring support for CSS preprocessors in Webpack is a standard task for modern frontend projects. It lets you use the preprocessors\' powerful features while keeping the build automated. This approach is used in most React, Vue and other SPA applications in order to simplify working with styles.","npm install --save-dev sass-loader sass css-loader style-loader\\n\\nmodule.exports = {\\n  module: {\\n    rules: [\\n      {\\n        test: /\\\\.scss$/,\\n        use: [\\n          \'style-loader\',\\n          \'css-loader\',\\n          \'sass-loader\'\\n        ]\\n      }\\n    ]\\n  }\\n};\\n\\nimport \'./styles.scss\';\\n\\nconst element = document.createElement(\'div\');\\nelement.className = \'container\';\\ndocument.body.appendChild(element);"],[534,"CSS blocks rendering because the browser does not begin painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file. This is necessary in order to avoid displaying the page without its styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content). The browser builds the DOM and the CSSOM, and rendering begins only once they have been combined into the Render Tree. To speed loading up you can use media queries or inline critical styles.","How CSS blocks rendering\\nWhen the browser loads a web page it goes through the Critical Rendering Path. That path comprises several stages: building the DOM (Document Object Model) from the HTML, building the CSSOM (CSS Object Model) from the CSS, combining them into the Render Tree, computing the geometry of the elements (Layout) and, finally, painting (Paint).\\nCSS counts as a render-blocking resource, because the browser does not begin painting the page until it has loaded and processed every CSS file listed in a `<link>` in the `<head>`. This is done in order to prevent the page from appearing without its styles (a Flash of Unstyled Content, FOUC). If the browser began rendering before the CSS had loaded, the user would first see unstyled content and then a sudden application of the styles, which makes the user experience worse.\\nAn example of the effect on performance\\nImagine you have an external CSS file that takes 2 seconds to load. For those 2 seconds the page will stay blank, even if the HTML has already loaded in full. This increases the time to the First Paint and makes Core Web Vitals metrics such as the First Contentful Paint (FCP) worse.\\n\\nHow to reduce the blocking\\n\\n- Use media queries: if CSS is applied only under particular conditions (for printing, for example), add a `media` attribute. The browser will load such CSS but will not let it block rendering if the condition is not met.\\n- Inline critical styles: pick out the styles needed to display the top part of the page (above the fold) and insert them directly in the `<head>` inside a `<style>` tag. Load the remaining styles asynchronously.\\n- Asynchronous loading: use JavaScript to load CSS dynamically after the initial render — by means of `rel=\\"preload\\"` and the `onload` attribute, for example.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding CSS render blocking is critically important to optimizing the performance of web pages. By applying techniques such as inline critical styles and the asynchronous loading of non-critical CSS, you can improve loading speed and the user experience considerably, especially on mobile devices and slow connections.","<!DOCTYPE html>\\n<html>\\n<head>\\n  <link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"styles.css\\"> <!-- It blocks rendering -->\\n</head>\\n<body>\\n  <h1>Hello, world!</h1>\\n</body>\\n</html>\\n\\n<link rel=\\"stylesheet\\" href=\\"print.css\\" media=\\"print\\"> <!-- It does not block rendering on screen -->\\n<link rel=\\"preload\\" href=\\"non-critical.css\\" as=\\"style\\" onload=\\"this.rel=\'stylesheet\'\\">"],[535,"text-overflow: ellipsis adds an ellipsis at the end of text that does not fit in its container. For it to work you must set overflow: hidden (or another value other than visible) and white-space: nowrap (so that the text does not wrap). You also need to give the container a fixed width.","What is text-overflow: ellipsis?\\nIt is a CSS property that lets you clip text that spills out past a block\'s bounds and add an ellipsis (...) at the end. It is often used for single-line headings, buttons or table cells, so that the text does not break the layout.\\nThe mandatory conditions\\nFor the property to work, three things have to be set:\\n\\n- overflow: hidden — it hides the content that spills out past the block\'s bounds.\\n- white-space: nowrap — it forbids the text from wrapping onto a new line.\\n- A fixed width — the container must have an explicitly set width (width, max-width or flex-basis).\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIf you remove any of these properties, the ellipsis will not appear — the text will either wrap or simply be clipped without an ellipsis.\\nConclusion\\ntext-overflow: ellipsis is a simple, reliable way of making single-line text responsive and tidy. Apply it in interfaces where it matters to keep the elements compact — in lists, cards or menus, for example.",".ellipsis-text {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n  white-space: nowrap;\\n  text-overflow: ellipsis;\\n}"],[536,"The white-space: nowrap property forbids text from wrapping onto a new line automatically. All of the text stays on one line until a `<br>` tag or a forced break is encountered. This is useful for buttons, headings, navigation items and table cells, where a line break is undesirable. If the container overflows, the text may spill out past its bounds, which is why it is often combined with overflow: hidden and text-overflow: ellipsis.","How white-space: nowrap works\\nThe `white-space` property governs the handling of spaces and line breaks in text. The value `nowrap` forbids the browser from wrapping text onto a new line automatically when it reaches the bounds of its parent element. All of the text stays on one line, ignoring the usual word wrapping. Forced line breaks (through `<br>` or a newline character in the HTML, for example) still work.\\nWhere it is used\\n\\n- Buttons and navigation links — so that the text does not break onto two lines.\\n- Headings and captions, where it matters to keep a phrase intact.\\n- Table cells, especially with a fixed width, in order to avoid ugly wrapping.\\n- Elements with an ellipsis (`text-overflow: ellipsis`) — `nowrap` is mandatory for that effect to work.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the text is clipped and an ellipsis is added if it does not fit in the 150px. Without `nowrap` the text would wrap onto the next line.\\nConclusion\\nUse `white-space: nowrap` when you need to guarantee that text stays on one line — in compact interfaces or tables, for example, or when creating the ellipsis effect. Remember the possibility of the container overflowing, however, and combine it with `overflow` where necessary.",".no-wrap {\\n  white-space: nowrap;\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n  text-overflow: ellipsis;\\n  width: 150px;\\n  border: 1px solid #ccc;\\n}\\n\\n<div class=\\"no-wrap\\">Some very long text that must not wrap</div>"],[537,"A dropdown list can be clipped because of the CSS property overflow: hidden on the table or on its parent container. The problem also arises if the list element has a low z-index or sits inside a container with a fixed height. The solution is to set overflow: visible on the table or to use position: fixed for the dropdown list.","The causes of a dropdown list being clipped\\nA dropdown list inside a table is often clipped because of the CSS property `overflow: hidden` applied to the table, to its cell or to a parent container. That property hides content that spills out past the element\'s bounds. The problem can also be caused by a low `z-index` on the dropdown list or by its sitting inside a container with a fixed height.\\nHow this works\\nTables in HTML have a strict structure, and their cells (`td` or `th`) do not by default let their content spill out past their bounds unless `overflow: visible` is set. When the dropdown list opens it may extend beyond the cell, but if `overflow: hidden` is set on the table or on its parent, part of the list will be hidden.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nThe solutions\\n\\n- Set `overflow: visible` on the table and its cells.\\n- Use `position: fixed` for the dropdown list, so that it is positioned relative to the browser window.\\n- Place the dropdown list outside the table by means of JavaScript and absolute positioning.\\nConclusion: the problem of a dropdown list being clipped inside a table is solved by configuring the overflow and positioning CSS properties correctly. This matters for building convenient interfaces, especially in forms and data tables.","<table style=\\"overflow: hidden;\\">\\n  <tr>\\n    <td>\\n      <div class=\\"dropdown\\">\\n        <button>Open</button>\\n        <div class=\\"dropdown-menu\\">\\n          <!-- the dropdown list -->\\n        </div>\\n      </div>\\n    </td>\\n  </tr>\\n</table>"],[538,"The overflow problem arises when a dropdown list extends beyond the bounds of its parent container. You can solve it by means of the CSS property overflow: visible on the parent, by using React portals (ReactDOM.createPortal) in order to render the list outside the container, or by applying libraries such as Popper.js for dynamic positioning. Setting a z-index and checking the bounds of the screen also helps.","Understanding the overflow problem\\nWhen a dropdown list is displayed inside a container with a limited height or with the property overflow: hidden, its content can be clipped. This is often encountered in modal windows, side panels or components with a fixed height. The problem is aggravated if the list has to appear beyond the bounds of its parent element.\\nThe main approaches to solving it\\n\\n- The CSS solution: set overflow: visible on the parent container, if that does not break the design. It can break the scrolling or the layout, however.\\n- React Portals: use ReactDOM.createPortal in order to render the dropdown list in a separate DOM node (in the body, for example). This guarantees that the list will not be constrained by its parent\'s styles.\\n- Positioning libraries: Popper.js or Floating UI compute the optimal position for the list automatically, avoiding both spilling out past the screen and overflow.\\nAn example with a portal\\n\\nIn this example the list is rendered in the body, so the parent\'s overflow does not affect it. It is also important to manage the z-index and the closing of the list when a click happens outside it.\\nAn example with Popper.js\\n\\nPopper.js adjusts the position automatically so that the list does not extend beyond the visible area.\\nConclusion\\nTo solve overflow in dropdown lists, use portals if you need to get rid of the parent\'s constraints entirely, or positioning libraries for dynamic adaptation. CSS solutions are suitable only for simple cases without deep nesting.","import ReactDOM from \'react-dom\';\\n\\nfunction Dropdown({ isOpen, children }) {\\n  if (!isOpen) return null;\\n  return ReactDOM.createPortal(\\n    <div className=\\"dropdown-menu\\">{children}</div>,\\n    document.body\\n  );\\n}\\n\\nimport { usePopper } from \'react-popper\';\\n\\nfunction Dropdown({ referenceElement }) {\\n  const [popperElement, setPopperElement] = useState(null);\\n  const { styles, attributes } = usePopper(referenceElement, popperElement);\\n\\n  return (\\n    <div ref={setPopperElement} style={styles.popper} {...attributes.popper}>\\n      The contents of the list\\n    </div>\\n  );\\n}"],[539,"Elements with display: block take up the whole available width of their parent and start on a new line. Elements with display: inline sit within the line and take up only as much room as they need. Block elements support width, height, margin and padding on every side, while inline ones support only horizontal spacing.","The main difference between display: block and display: inline\\nIn CSS every element can be given a display type through the display property. The two most basic values — block and inline — determine how the element behaves in the document flow.\\nBlock elements (block)\\nAn element with display: block takes up the whole available width of its parent container, unless an explicit width is set. It always starts on a new line, and the element that follows it will also be on a new line. Block elements support every box-model property: width, height, margin, padding (which work on every side).\\n\\nInline elements (inline)\\nAn element with display: inline does not break the line — it sits on the same line as its neighbouring inline elements. Its width and height are determined by its content, and the width and height properties are ignored. Vertical margin and padding do not affect the neighbouring elements, although padding can visually extend past the bounds.\\n\\nPractical use\\nBlock elements (div, p, h1–h6) are used for the structure of a page — headings, paragraphs, sections. Inline ones (span, a, strong) are used to format text within a line. If you need to combine the properties of both types, display: inline-block is used.\\nConclusion: understanding the difference between block and inline is necessary in order to lay things out correctly and to control the document flow. Use block for large structural blocks and inline for styling text within a line.","<div style=\\"display: block; width: 200px; margin: 10px;\\">Block 1</div>\\n<div style=\\"display: block;\\">Block 2</div>\\n\\n<span style=\\"display: inline; padding: 5px;\\">Text 1</span>\\n<span style=\\"display: inline;\\">Text 2</span>"],[540,"The box-sizing property governs the computation of an element\'s size. The value content-box (the default) adds the padding and the border to the width and the height, making the element bigger. The value border-box includes the padding and the border in the dimensions you set, which simplifies layout. This is especially useful for responsive grids.","What is box-sizing?\\nThe `box-sizing` property in CSS determines how the browser computes an element\'s total width and height. It directly affects whether the inner spacing (`padding`) and the borders (`border`) are included in the dimensions you set. Understanding this property is necessary for precise control over a layout, especially when building responsive interfaces.\\nThe property\'s values\\n\\n- content-box (the default value): the width and the height are set for the content alone. `padding` and `border` are added on top, increasing the element\'s final size.\\n- border-box: the width and the height include the content, the `padding` and the `border`. The element\'s final size stays equal to what you set, which simplifies the calculations.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhere it is used\\nThe `border-box` value is widely used in modern CSS frameworks (Bootstrap, for example) and when laying out responsive grids. It lets you set column widths as percentages without worrying that `padding` or `border` will break the grid. It is recommended that you set `box-sizing: border-box` globally for every element through the `*` selector.\\nConclusion\\nUse `box-sizing: border-box` in order to simplify the computation of element sizes, especially in responsive layouts. It makes layout more predictable and reduces the number of mistakes when adding spacing and borders.","/* content-box (the default) */\\n.element-content {\\n  box-sizing: content-box;\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px;\\n  border: 5px solid black;\\n  /* The final width: 200 + 20*2 + 5*2 = 250px */\\n}\\n\\n/* border-box */\\n.element-border {\\n  box-sizing: border-box;\\n  width: 200px;\\n  padding: 20px;\\n  border: 5px solid black;\\n  /* The final width: 200px (the padding and the border are inside) */\\n}"],[541,"The `<details>` tag does not support animating the opening/closing through a CSS transition or animation, since its height changes instantly. For a smooth animation you have to use JavaScript, which complicates the code. In addition, `<details>` does not let you manage the state of several elements at once, which is what an accordion needs.","Why is `<details>` not suitable for an animated accordion?\\nThe `<details>` tag is a native HTML element for creating expandable content. Its behaviour does not meet the requirements of an animated accordion, however, for several reasons.\\nThe absence of CSS animation\\nBrowsers do not support animating the `height` or `max-height` property on `<details>`, since its expansion happens instantly. A CSS transition does not fire, because the change of state is not gradual.\\n\\nManaging the state\\nAn accordion requires that opening one element closes the others. `<details>` provides no built-in mechanism for that — you have to use JavaScript to manage the `open` attribute on every element.\\n\\nThe styling limitations\\nStyling `<details>` is limited: you cannot easily change the icon or animate the marker without additional CSS tricks. That makes creating a uniform design harder.\\nConclusion\\nUse `<details>` for simple expandable blocks without animation. For an accordion with smooth opening/closing and synchronized states, it is better to implement a custom component in JavaScript with a CSS transition.","/* The animation does not work */\\ndetails {\\n  transition: height 0.3s ease;\\n}\\ndetails[open] {\\n  height: auto;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of managing it by hand\\nconst details = document.querySelectorAll(\'details\');\\ndetails.forEach(d => {\\n  d.addEventListener(\'toggle\', () => {\\n    details.forEach(other => {\\n      if (other !== d) other.removeAttribute(\'open\');\\n    });\\n  });\\n});"],[542,"An accordion without JavaScript is implemented by means of a hidden checkbox and a label. The checkbox holds the open/closed state, and the CSS selector :checked governs the display of the content. For the animation a CSS transition on the max-height or transform property is used. This is a simple, accessible way of creating interactive elements without scripts.","The principle of a CSS accordion\\nAn accordion without JavaScript is built on the \\"checkbox hack\\": a hidden `<input type=\\"checkbox\\">` element holds the state (open/closed), and the `<label>` associated with it serves as the visible button. When the user clicks the label, the checkbox toggles, and the CSS selector `:checked` applies styles to the sibling elements, showing or hiding the content.\\nThe basic HTML structure\\n\\nThe CSS for the animation\\nFor smooth opening, the `max-height` property with a transition is used. Initially the content is hidden (`max-height: 0; overflow: hidden`), and when the checkbox is activated it expands to a large value.\\n\\nUses and limitations\\nThis approach is suited to simple interfaces, where no complex logic or multiple open panels are needed. For an accordion with a single open section you can use `<input type=\\"radio\\">` with the same name. The method does not support animating the height precisely, however (because of the fixed max-height), and it is awkward for dynamic content.\\nConclusion\\nA pure-CSS accordion is an excellent solution for static pages, where performance and the absence of any dependence on JavaScript matter. It is simple to implement but limited in flexibility and in the precision of its animation.","<div class=\\"accordion\\">\\n  <input type=\\"checkbox\\" id=\\"item1\\" class=\\"accordion-input\\">\\n  <label for=\\"item1\\" class=\\"accordion-label\\">Heading 1</label>\\n  <div class=\\"accordion-content\\">\\n    <p>The contents of the panel.</p>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n.accordion-content {\\n  max-height: 0;\\n  overflow: hidden;\\n  transition: max-height 0.3s ease;\\n}\\n\\n.accordion-input:checked ~ .accordion-content {\\n  max-height: 200px; /* a sufficiently large value */\\n}"],[543,"An attached property is a special kind of dependency property that is defined in one class but can be set on any other element. It lets child elements set the values of properties that affect their placement or behaviour within a parent container. The Grid.Row property, for example, sets the grid row for an element, even though the property itself is defined in the Grid class.","What are attached properties?\\nAttached properties are a mechanism in WPF that lets one element set the values of properties defined in another class. It is an extension of the dependency-properties system, where a property is declared in one type but can be set on any object that supports dependency properties.\\nHow does it work?\\nAttached properties are implemented through static Set and Get methods, which call the internal DependencyObject.SetValue and GetValue methods. In XAML they are written in the form `OwnerClass.PropertyName`. The `Grid.Row` property, for example, lets you set the grid row for any child element.\\n\\nAn example of its use in XAML\\n\\nHere `Grid.Row` and `Grid.Column` are attached properties that determine the button\'s position in the grid.\\nWhere is it used?\\nAttached properties are widely used in layout controls (Grid, Canvas, DockPanel), and also in styles and triggers. They let you create flexible, reusable components without disturbing the class hierarchy.\\nConclusion: attached properties are a powerful WPF tool for dividing logic between parent and child elements. They are worth applying when you need to set an element\'s behaviour or placement in the context of another container without changing the element itself.","// Defining an attached property\\npublic static readonly DependencyProperty RowProperty =\\n    DependencyProperty.RegisterAttached(\\n        \\"Row\\",\\n        typeof(int),\\n        typeof(Grid),\\n        new FrameworkPropertyMetadata(0));\\n\\npublic static void SetRow(UIElement element, int value)\\n{\\n    element.SetValue(RowProperty, value);\\n}\\n\\npublic static int GetRow(UIElement element)\\n{\\n    return (int)element.GetValue(RowProperty);\\n}\\n\\n<Grid>\\n    <Button Grid.Row=\\"1\\" Grid.Column=\\"2\\" Content=\\"Click\\" />\\n</Grid>"],[544,"No, changing the transform or translate properties does not cause a Reflow. These properties are handled at the compositing stage, bypassing the Layout and Paint stages. That makes them ideal for animations, since they do not recompute the geometry of the other elements and do not cause expensive repaints.","What is Reflow and how does it work?\\nReflow (or Layout) is the rendering stage at which the browser computes the geometry of the elements: their dimensions, their position and their placement relative to other elements. Reflow is triggered by changes that affect the page\'s layout — a change of width, height, margin, padding, display, positioning (top, left) and so on. It is an expensive operation, since it can touch both child and parent elements.\\nWhy does transform/translate not cause a Reflow?\\nThe transform and translate properties (along with opacity, filter and will-change) are handled at the compositing stage. The browser creates a separate layer for the element (a compositing layer) and moves it by means of the GPU, without recomputing Layout and Paint. That means a change of transform does not affect the position of the other elements in the document flow — the element as it were \\"floats\\" above the page.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen does this matter?\\nWhen creating animations, especially at 60fps, using transform instead of left/top is critically important to performance. It is standard practice in CSS animations and in JavaScript libraries (GreenSock, Framer Motion, for example).\\nConclusion: use transform/translate for movement animations, in order to avoid Reflow and ensure the interface runs smoothly, especially on mobile devices and with complex layouts.","// Bad: it causes a Reflow on every frame of the animation\\nconst el = document.getElementById(\'box\');\\nel.style.left = \'100px\'; // Reflow!\\n\\n// Good: it causes no Reflow and uses compositing\\nel.style.transform = \'translateX(100px)\'; // No Reflow"],[545,"A chart\'s theme in Chart.js can be customized through the global Chart.defaults settings, overriding the colours, the fonts and the styles. You can also use the built-in plugins, such as Chart.plugins.register, or create your own. For individual charts the settings are passed in the options object when the instance is created.","The main ways of customizing a theme in Chart.js\\nChart.js provides flexible means of changing the appearance of charts, which lets you adapt them to a corporate style or to specific design requirements. Customization can be done at several levels: globally, for a particular chart, or through plugins.\\nConfiguring the theme globally\\nThe global settings are set through the `Chart.defaults` object. This lets you change the colours, the fonts and other parameters for every chart on the page. For example:\\n\\nConfiguring a particular chart\\nWhen creating a chart you can pass an `options` object with individual settings. That overrides the global values. An example:\\n\\nUsing plugins\\nChart.js supports plugins that can change the theme. The `chartjs-plugin-datalabels` plugin, for example, lets you configure the data labels. You can also create a plugin of your own for unique styles:\\n\\nConclusion\\nCustomizing the theme in Chart.js is done through the global settings, through a particular chart\'s parameters, or through plugins. This lets you control the appearance of your visualizations flexibly, bringing them into line with the design and brand requirements.","Chart.defaults.color = \'#333\';\\nChart.defaults.font.family = \'Arial\';\\nChart.defaults.borderColor = \'#ccc\';\\n\\nconst ctx = document.getElementById(\'myChart\').getContext(\'2d\');\\nnew Chart(ctx, {\\n  type: \'bar\',\\n  data: { ... },\\n  options: {\\n    plugins: {\\n      legend: {\\n        labels: {\\n          color: \'#ff0000\'\\n        }\\n      }\\n    },\\n    scales: {\\n      y: {\\n        grid: {\\n          color: \'#e0e0e0\'\\n        }\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n});\\n\\nChart.register({\\n  id: \'customTheme\',\\n  beforeDraw: function(chart) {\\n    const ctx = chart.ctx;\\n    ctx.fillStyle = \'#f5f5f5\';\\n    ctx.fillRect(0, 0, chart.width, chart.height);\\n  }\\n});"],[546,"A native mobile application is built specifically for one platform, such as iOS or Android, using that platform\'s own languages and tools (Swift/Objective-C for iOS, Kotlin/Java for Android). It has direct access to the device\'s hardware capabilities, which gives maximum performance and a smooth user interface. Such applications are installed through the official app stores and can use every feature of the OS.","What is a native mobile application?\\nA native mobile application is a program developed to run on a particular operating system, such as iOS or Android, using the official programming languages and tools the platform provides. For iOS that is Swift or Objective-C and the Xcode environment; for Android — Kotlin or Java and Android Studio. The main advantage of the native approach is full integration with the device and its capabilities.\\nThe key characteristics\\n\\n- Direct access to the hardware components: the camera, GPS, the accelerometer, the sensors.\\n- High performance, since the code is compiled for the specific architecture.\\n- The use of every OS capability: push notifications, widgets, background tasks.\\n- Conformity with the platform\'s guidelines (the Human Interface Guidelines for iOS, Material Design for Android).\\nAn example of the code (iOS, Swift)\\n\\nAn example of the code (Android, Kotlin)\\n\\nWhen should native development be used?\\nThe native approach is ideal for applications that need high performance (games, graphics editors), deep integration with the device (fitness trackers, cameras) or the use of platform-specific features. It is also preferable if a flawless interface and conformity with the platform\'s standards matter. Native development does, however, require a separate codebase for each platform, which increases the time and the cost of development.\\nConclusion: native applications give the best user experience and performance, but they require more resources than cross-platform solutions do.","import UIKit\\n\\nclass ViewController: UIViewController {\\n    override func viewDidLoad() {\\n        super.viewDidLoad()\\n        // Setting the interface up\\n        let label = UILabel()\\n        label.text = \\"Hello, world!\\"\\n        label.textAlignment = .center\\n        view.addSubview(label)\\n    }\\n}\\n\\nimport android.os.Bundle\\nimport androidx.appcompat.app.AppCompatActivity\\nimport android.widget.TextView\\n\\nclass MainActivity : AppCompatActivity() {\\n    override fun onCreate(savedInstanceState: Bundle?) {\\n        super.onCreate(savedInstanceState)\\n        setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)\\n        val textView = findViewById<TextView>(R.id.textView)\\n        textView.text = \\"Hello, world!\\"\\n    }\\n}"],[547,"The opacity property can cause a repaint and compositing, which loads the CPU. For animations it is better to use opacity together with will-change or transform, in order to bring the GPU into play. Avoid changing opacity frequently on large elements without hardware acceleration.","The effect of opacity on performance\\nThe `opacity` property governs an element\'s transparency. When it changes, the browser may perform a repaint and compositing, which takes CPU resources. If the element has no hardware acceleration, every change of opacity causes a full repaint, especially on complex pages.\\nHow to optimize it\\n\\n- Use `will-change: opacity` or `transform: translateZ(0)` in order to move the element onto a separate layer and bring the GPU into play.\\n- Animate opacity together with transform for smooth transitions without a repaint.\\n- Avoid changing opacity frequently on large elements with a great many nested child elements.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse `will-change` or `transform` for opacity animations, in order to reduce the load on the CPU and ensure smooth rendering. This is especially important on mobile devices and on pages with a great many animated elements.","/* Bad: without acceleration */\\n.element {\\n  opacity: 0.5;\\n  transition: opacity 0.3s;\\n}\\n\\n/* Good: with hardware acceleration */\\n.element {\\n  opacity: 0.5;\\n  will-change: opacity;\\n  transition: opacity 0.3s;\\n}\\n\\n/* An alternative through transform */\\n.element {\\n  transform: translateZ(0);\\n  opacity: 0.5;\\n  transition: opacity 0.3s;\\n}"],[548,"For a child element with position: absolute to be positioned relative to its parent, the parent element has to be given position: relative (or any other value except static). This creates a new positioning context, and the child element will measure its coordinates from the parent\'s bounds.","How absolute positioning works\\nAn element with `position: absolute` is torn out of the normal document flow and positioned relative to its nearest ancestor whose `position` property is set to `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed` or `sticky` (not `static`). If there is no such ancestor, the element is positioned relative to the initial containing block — usually the browser window.\\nWhat has to be specified on the parent\\nIt is enough to give the parent `position: relative`. That will not change its own position on the page, but it will make it the point of reference for the absolutely positioned child. You can also use `position: absolute` or `fixed` on the parent, but that will affect its own behaviour.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the child block will be offset 20px from the top and 30px from the left of the parent\'s bounds.\\nConclusion\\nUse `position: relative` on the parent when you need to place a child element precisely inside it without disturbing the flow of the other elements. This is the basis for building complex layouts, tooltips, modal windows and dropdown menus.","<div class=\\"parent\\">\\n  <div class=\\"child\\">I am positioned relative to my parent</div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n.parent {\\n  position: relative; /* the key property */\\n  width: 300px;\\n  height: 200px;\\n  background: lightgray;\\n}\\n.child {\\n  position: absolute;\\n  top: 20px;\\n  left: 30px;\\n  background: coral;\\n}\\n</style>"],[549,"Composite layers are the separate layers into which the browser divides a page in order to speed rendering up. They let animations and transforms be handled on the GPU without repainting the whole document. This improves performance, especially with frequent changes — when using transform or opacity, for example.","What are composite layers?\\nComposite layers are part of the browser\'s rendering process, in which a page is divided into several independent layers. Each layer can be processed separately and then combined (compositing) into the final image. This lets the browser repaint only the layers that have changed rather than the whole page, which speeds things up considerably.\\nHow do they affect performance?\\nThe use of composite layers is especially useful for animations and for elements that change often. When animating the `transform` or `opacity` property, for example, the browser can move the element onto a separate layer and handle it on the GPU, avoiding an expensive repaint and reflow. That makes animations smooth and reduces the load on the CPU.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen should they be used?\\nComposite layers are useful for elements with frequent animations — modal windows, carousels or parallax effects, for example. Creating too many layers can lead to increased memory consumption, however, so use them deliberately, only for the critical elements.\\nConclusion: composite layers are a powerful tool for optimizing the performance of CSS animations and transforms, but they should be applied judiciously, so as not to overload the device\'s memory.","/* Creating a composite layer through will-change */\\n.element {\\n  will-change: transform, opacity;\\n}\\n\\n/* Or through a 3D transform */\\n.element {\\n  transform: translateZ(0);\\n}\\n\\n/* An animation on a separate layer */\\n@keyframes slide {\\n  from { transform: translateX(0); }\\n  to { transform: translateX(100px); }\\n}\\n.animated {\\n  animation: slide 2s infinite;\\n}"],[550,"Transform: translate() triggers compositing on the GPU without causing a reflow or a repaint. Changing position: absolute requires the geometry to be recomputed (reflow) and repainted, which loads the CPU. translate is therefore faster and smoother, especially for animations.","How the browser renders a page\\nThe browser goes through several stages: style, geometry computation (layout), painting (paint) and compositing (composite). Changing position: absolute touches layout — the browser recomputes the position of the element and of all of its neighbours. That causes a reflow (recomputation) and a repaint, which loads the CPU.\\nWhy transform is faster\\nTransform: translate() does not change the element\'s geometry in the document flow. The browser puts the element on a separate layer (a compositing layer) and hands it to the GPU. The GPU simply shifts a ready-made texture without recomputing layout and paint. This is called a composite-only animation.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen does this matter\\nFor animations that have to run at 60 fps (parallax, carousels, drag-and-drop, for example). For static positioning the difference is not noticeable. Use transform for movement and position for static layout.\\nConclusion: transform: translate() uses GPU acceleration and avoids the expensive reflow/repaint operations, so it is preferable for smooth animations and for interfaces with a high frame rate.","// The slow option — it causes a reflow\\n.element {\\n  position: absolute;\\n  left: 100px;\\n  top: 50px;\\n  transition: left 0.3s;\\n}\\n.element:hover {\\n  left: 200px;\\n}\\n\\n// The fast option — compositing only\\n.element {\\n  transform: translate(100px, 50px);\\n  transition: transform 0.3s;\\n}\\n.element:hover {\\n  transform: translate(200px, 50px);\\n}"],[551,"The prefers-reduced-motion media query lets you determine whether the user has turned on the reduce-motion setting in their system. It is used to disable or replace animations, so as to avoid discomfort for people with vestibular disorders. This improves the accessibility of web applications.","What is prefers-reduced-motion?\\nThe `prefers-reduced-motion` media query is a CSS feature that checks the user\'s system setting for reducing animation. It returns one of two values: `no-preference` (the default) or `reduce` (the user has asked for motion to be minimized). This query is part of the CSS Media Queries Level 5 specification and is aimed at improving the accessibility of web content.\\nWhy is it needed?\\nMany people experience discomfort, dizziness or nausea when viewing intense animation, especially those with vestibular disorders or migraines. By using `prefers-reduced-motion`, developers can disable or replace animations, making a site more comfortable for such users. It also conforms to the WCAG (Web Content Accessibility Guidelines) recommendations.\\nHow do you use it?\\nThe query is used in CSS or in JavaScript. In CSS you can override your animations for users who prefer reduced motion. An example:\\n\\nIn JavaScript you can check the setting through `window.matchMedia`:\\n\\nWhere should it be used?\\nThis media query is useful on any site with animation: parallax effects, rotating elements, flashing banners, page transitions. It is especially important for sites with a great deal of movement — portfolios or landing pages, for example.\\nConclusion: use `prefers-reduced-motion` in order to build an inclusive interface that respects the needs of users sensitive to motion. It improves accessibility and makes the user experience better.","/* The ordinary animation */\\n.element {\\n  animation: spin 2s infinite;\\n}\\n\\n/* We disable the animation for users with the reduce setting */\\n@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {\\n  .element {\\n    animation: none;\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nconst motionQuery = window.matchMedia(\'(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)\');\\nif (motionQuery.matches) {\\n  // We disable the animations\\n}"],[552,"To disable animations and transforms outside the viewport, use the CSS property `will-change` with care, and apply `content-visibility: auto` to off-screen elements. This lets the browser skip the rendering of the invisible parts of the page, saving resources. You can also use JavaScript to check visibility through an Intersection Observer and disable animations dynamically.","Optimizing animations outside the viewport\\nWhen elements lie outside the visible area of the screen, their animations and transforms can still consume processor and graphics resources. The browser goes on computing the changes, even though the user cannot see them. This is especially critical for pages with a great many animated elements — in news feeds or galleries, for example.\\nThe main approaches\\n\\n- The CSS property content-visibility: set `content-visibility: auto` on elements outside the viewport. The browser will skip rendering them until the moment they become visible. An example:\\n\\n- The Intersection Observer API: use JavaScript to track an element\'s visibility and turn its animations on and off dynamically. An example:\\n\\n- The will-change property: use it with care, since it can increase memory consumption. It is better to apply it only to elements that are going to be animated in the near future.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose you have a list of 1000 elements with an appearance animation. Without optimization the browser will compute the animations for every element, even if only 10 are visible. Using `content-visibility` and an Intersection Observer, you can cut the load by 90%.\\nConclusion\\nApply these techniques to pages with long lists, infinite scrolling or complex animations, in order to improve performance and loading time. This is especially useful on mobile devices and weak systems.",".lazy-element {\\n  content-visibility: auto;\\n  contain-intrinsic-size: 500px;\\n}\\n\\nconst observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {\\n  entries.forEach(entry => {\\n    if (entry.isIntersecting) {\\n      entry.target.classList.add(\'animate\');\\n    } else {\\n      entry.target.classList.remove(\'animate\');\\n    }\\n  });\\n});\\nobserver.observe(element);"],[553,"The position property in CSS determines how an element is placed in the document. static — the standard flow. relative — an offset from its ordinary place. absolute — positioning relative to the nearest positioned ancestor. fixed — fixed relative to the browser window. sticky — a hybrid of relative and fixed; it sticks when scrolling reaches the specified threshold.","The basics of positioning in CSS\\nThe position property governs how an element is placed on a page. By default every element has position: static and follows the normal document flow. Changing position to another value lets you tear the element out of the flow or change its anchor point.\\nThe types of position\\n\\n- static — the standard behaviour. The element is placed in the order in which it appears in the HTML. The top, left, right and bottom properties have no effect.\\n- relative — the element stays in the flow, but it can be offset from its original place. The neighbouring elements do not respond to the offset.\\n- absolute — the element is removed from the flow and positioned relative to its nearest ancestor whose position is not static (or to the body, if there is none). It is set through top/left/right/bottom.\\n- fixed — the element is fixed relative to the browser window and does not move as the page scrolls. It is often used for headers or buttons.\\n- sticky — a combination of relative and fixed. The element behaves like relative until it reaches the given threshold (top: 0, for example), after which it sticks to the screen like fixed.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding position is necessary for building complex layouts, modal windows, toolbars and responsive interfaces. The choice of type depends on the task: absolute for dropdown menus, fixed for a header, sticky for sections that stick.","<div class=\\"container\\">\\n  <div class=\\"box relative\\">Relative</div>\\n  <div class=\\"box absolute\\">Absolute</div>\\n  <div class=\\"box fixed\\">Fixed</div>\\n  <div class=\\"box sticky\\">Sticky</div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n  .container { position: relative; height: 200vh; }\\n  .box { width: 100px; height: 100px; }\\n  .relative { position: relative; top: 20px; left: 20px; }\\n  .absolute { position: absolute; top: 50px; right: 0; }\\n  .fixed { position: fixed; bottom: 10px; right: 10px; }\\n  .sticky { position: sticky; top: 0; }\\n</style>"],[554,"By default flex-grow is 0, flex-shrink is 1 and flex-basis is auto. That means flex items will not stretch but can shrink, and their initial size is determined by their content or by the width they have been given.","The default values of flex-grow, flex-shrink and flex-basis\\nIn CSS Flexbox each of a flex item\'s properties has a default value that determines how the items behave in the container. Understanding these values matters for predictable layout behaviour.\\nThe default values\\n\\n- flex-grow: 0 — the item will not grow in order to fill the free space.\\n- flex-shrink: 1 — the item can shrink if there is not enough room.\\n- flex-basis: auto — the item\'s initial size is determined by its content or by the width/height property.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHow it works\\nWhen you do not set these properties, flex items behave like this: they do not stretch, but they can shrink in order to fit in the container. If the container is too narrow, for example, the items will shrink proportionally. If there is plenty of room, they will stay at their own size.\\nConclusion\\nThe default values are suitable for most cases where you want the items to adjust to the container without any excess stretching. This is the basis for building responsive layouts.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n}\\n\\n.item {\\n  /* The default values */\\n  flex-grow: 0;\\n  flex-shrink: 1;\\n  flex-basis: auto;\\n}"],[555,"The flex-basis property sets a flex item\'s initial size along the main axis, before flex-grow and flex-shrink are applied. It can be given in pixels, in percentages or as a keyword such as auto. If the value is auto, the size is taken from the element\'s width or height. If a specific value is given, it overrides width/height.","What is flex-basis?\\nThe `flex-basis` property in CSS Flexbox determines a flex item\'s initial size along the main axis (horizontal for `flex-direction: row`, vertical for `column`). That size is used as the starting point before the browser applies the `flex-grow` and `flex-shrink` properties in order to distribute the free space.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen you set `flex-basis`, you are specifying the size you want for the element. If there is enough free room in the container, the element can grow (if `flex-grow > 0`). If there is not enough room, the element can shrink (if `flex-shrink > 0`). The value `auto` means the size is taken from the element\'s `width` or `height`. If a specific value is given (`200px`, for example), it overrides `width`/`height`.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example every element with the class `.item` will have an initial width of 150 pixels. If free room remains in the container, the elements will grow evenly. If there is not enough room, they will shrink.\\nWhere is it used?\\n`flex-basis` is often used to create responsive layouts where the items have to have a particular minimum size but can stretch or shrink according to the space available — in product cards or navigation bars, for example.\\nConclusion: use `flex-basis` when you need to set a flex item\'s initial size while still letting it change flexibly according to its container. It is a key property for building responsive interfaces without rigid fixed sizes.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n}\\n\\n.item {\\n  flex-basis: 150px; /* an initial width of 150px */\\n  flex-grow: 1;      /* it can grow */\\n  flex-shrink: 1;    /* it can shrink */\\n}"],[556,"The flex-grow property sets a flex item\'s growth factor relative to the other items in the container. If every item is given flex-grow: 1, they will take up equal space. An item with flex-grow: 2 will be twice as big as one with flex-grow: 1. The default value is 0, and the item does not grow.","What is flex-grow?\\nThe `flex-grow` property in CSS Flexbox determines how a flex item will grow in size when there is free space in the container. It takes a non-negative number that sets the item\'s growth factor relative to the other items.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen a flex container has additional space (when the container\'s width is greater than the sum of every item\'s width, for example), that space is distributed between the items in proportion to their `flex-grow`. If every item is given `flex-grow: 1`, they will receive equal shares of the free space. An item with `flex-grow: 2` will receive twice as much additional space as an item with `flex-grow: 1`.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example a container 600px wide holds three items. If their base sizes are 100px each, the total width of the items is 300px and the free space is 300px. It is divided into 4 parts (1+2+1). The first item will receive 1 part (75px), the second 2 parts (150px) and the third 1 part (75px). The final widths: 175px, 250px, 175px.\\nWhere is it used?\\n`flex-grow` is often used to create responsive layouts where the items have to take up the available space — in toolbars, product cards or navigation menus, for example. It is a key property for building flexible, responsive interfaces.\\nConclusion: `flex-grow` lets you control the distribution of free space between flex items, making layouts more flexible and more responsive. Use it when you want the items to expand automatically in order to fill the container.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  width: 600px;\\n}\\n\\n.item1 {\\n  flex-grow: 1;\\n  background: lightblue;\\n}\\n\\n.item2 {\\n  flex-grow: 2;\\n  background: lightcoral;\\n}\\n\\n.item3 {\\n  flex-grow: 1;\\n  background: lightgreen;\\n}"],[557,"Margin is the outer spacing, which creates room between an element\'s border and the neighbouring elements. Padding is the inner spacing, which creates room between an element\'s content and its border. Margins can collapse (on adjacent elements), whereas padding cannot. A margin can be negative, whereas padding cannot.","The main difference between margin and padding\\nIn the CSS box model every element consists of its content, its inner spacing (padding), its border and its outer spacing (margin). Padding is the room between an element\'s content and its border. It increases the element\'s visible area, affecting its size. Margin is the room around an element\'s border, which separates it from the other elements. It does not affect the element\'s own size but does affect its position on the page.\\nThe key characteristics\\n\\n- Margin collapsing: the vertical margins of adjacent block elements can be merged (collapsed) into one equal to the larger of them. Padding never collapses.\\n- Negative values: a margin can be negative, which lets you overlap elements or shift them. Padding cannot be negative.\\n- Background and clickability: padding inherits the element\'s background and its click area, whereas a margin does not.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the padding will add 20px of room inside the element, increasing its total width to 244px (200 + 20*2 + 2*2). The margin will create 30px of empty space around the border, pushing the element away from its neighbours.\\nConclusion\\nUse padding when you need to create room inside an element (spacing between text and the edge of a button, for example). Use margin when you need to control the distance between elements (the spacing between cards, for example). Understanding this difference is critical to building predictable, responsive layouts.",".box {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  height: 100px;\\n  background: lightblue;\\n  padding: 20px;  /* inner spacing; it increases the size */\\n  margin: 30px;   /* outer spacing; it creates distance */\\n  border: 2px solid black;\\n}"],[558,"The :nth-child() pseudo-class selects elements by their position among all of their parent\'s child elements. li:nth-child(2), for example, will select the second item of the list. You can use formulas: :nth-child(3n+1), for example, will select every third element starting from the first. :nth-last-child() works in the same way, but the counting goes from the end.","The basics of the nth-child and nth-last-child pseudo-classes\\nThe `:nth-child()` pseudo-class in CSS lets you select elements on the basis of their ordinal number among all of their parent container\'s child elements. It is a powerful tool for styling lists, tables, grids and other repeating structures without having to add extra classes or JavaScript.\\nThe syntax and examples\\nIn the parentheses you can give:\\n\\n- A specific number: `:nth-child(3)` — the third element.\\n- Keywords: `odd` and `even`.\\n- A formula of the form `an + b`, where `a` is the step and `b` the offset. `3n+1`, for example, is every third element starting from the first.\\n\\nIn this example the first and the fourth element (3*0+1 and 3*1+1) will be shown in red, and the second-to-last element in yellow.\\nPractical use\\nThese pseudo-classes are often used for:\\n\\n- Styling the even/odd rows of tables in order to improve readability.\\n- Creating patterns in grids (every third element with spacing, for example).\\n- Highlighting the first or the last element in a group.\\nIt is important to remember that `:nth-child()` counts every child element, not only the elements of a particular type. If you need to count only elements with a particular tag, use `:nth-of-type()`.\\nConclusion: the `:nth-child()` and `:nth-last-child()` pseudo-classes are indispensable tools for styling repeating elements flexibly and concisely without changing the HTML structure. They are especially useful when working with dynamic lists and tables, where the number of elements can change.","<ul>\\n  <li>First</li>\\n  <li>Second</li>\\n  <li>Third</li>\\n  <li>Fourth</li>\\n  <li>Fifth</li>\\n</ul>\\n\\n<style>\\n  li:nth-child(3n+1) {\\n    color: red;\\n  }\\n  li:nth-last-child(2) {\\n    background: yellow;\\n  }\\n</style>"],[559,"The !important flag raises a CSS property\'s priority, but it can be overridden by another property with !important whose selector has a higher specificity. You can also use an inline style with !important, or add a rule with the same selector later in the code. Overusing !important does, however, make styles harder to maintain.","What !important is and how to work with it\\nThe `!important` flag in CSS is used to force a style to be applied, ignoring the usual rules of specificity. It can be overridden, however, by another rule with `!important` whose selector has a higher specificity or which comes later in the code.\\nThe ways of overriding it\\n\\n- Use a selector with a higher specificity (an ID rather than a class, for example) together with `!important`.\\n- Add an inline style with `!important` (the highest priority).\\n- Place the rule with `!important` after the original one in the stylesheet.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the inline style with `!important` will override all of the others, since it has the highest priority. If there is no inline style, the rule with the ID `#main .button` will take effect.\\nConclusion\\nUse `!important` only in extreme cases — to override styles from third-party libraries, for example. In ordinary practice it is better to rely on the specificity of your selectors and on the CSS cascade, in order to avoid confusion and make the code easier to maintain.","/* The original rule */\\n.button {\\n  color: red !important;\\n}\\n\\n/* Overriding it through a more specific selector */\\n#main .button {\\n  color: blue !important;\\n}\\n\\n/* An inline style */\\n<button class=\\"button\\" style=\\"color: green !important;\\">A button</button>"],[560,"Inline styles have a higher priority than ordinary CSS classes, because of the rules of CSS specificity. An inline style\'s specificity counts as the highest among the simple selectors, so it overrides properties set through classes. The exception is the use of !important, which can beat an inline style if it is applied in a class.","What is CSS specificity?\\nSpecificity is the mechanism that determines which styles will be applied to an element when several rules conflict. It is computed from the types of selector: inline styles have the greatest weight, then ID selectors, then classes and attributes, and finally tags and pseudo-elements.\\nThe priority of inline styles\\nInline styles, set through the `style` attribute in the HTML, have a specificity of 1000 (notionally), which is higher than a class\'s (10). That means any property specified inline will override the same property from a class, unless the class uses `!important`.\\nAn example\\n\\nThe exception: !important\\nIf you add `!important` in the class, it will override the inline style, since `!important` has the highest priority of all the rules, inline included.\\n\\nConclusion\\nInline styles are convenient for quick overrides, but overusing them makes the code harder to maintain. It is better to use classes and to avoid `!important`, in order to keep the cascade predictable.","<div class=\\"box\\" style=\\"color: red;\\">Text</div>\\n\\n.box {\\n  color: blue;\\n}\\n// The result: the text will be red, since the inline style takes priority\\n\\n.box {\\n  color: blue !important;\\n}\\n// Now the text will be blue, despite the inline style"],[561,"Inline styles have a high specificity, but they can be overridden by means of the !important rule in CSS. You can also change the style attribute through JavaScript. Another way is to use the CSS property all: revert, which resets inline styles.","What inline styles are and why they are hard to override\\nInline styles are set directly in an HTML element\'s style attribute. They have a very high specificity (1000 points), so ordinary CSS rules cannot override them without special techniques.\\nThe main ways of overriding them\\n\\n- !important — added to a CSS property in order to raise its priority above inline styles. For example:\\n\\nwill override `style=\\"color: red\\"`.\\n- JavaScript — you can change or remove the style attribute directly:\\n\\n- The CSS all: revert — it resets every inline style to the inherited or initial values:\\n\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example the colour will become blue because of the !important, while the font size will stay 20px, since an ordinary rule cannot override an inline one.\\nConclusion\\nUse !important only in extreme cases, when an inline style absolutely has to be overridden — in component libraries, for example. For flexible control over styles it is better to avoid inline attributes and to use CSS classes.","div { color: blue !important; }\\n\\nelement.style.color = \'green\'; // or element.removeAttribute(\'style\');\\n\\ndiv { all: revert; }\\n\\n<div style=\\"color: red; font-size: 20px;\\">Text</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n  div {\\n    color: blue !important; /* it will override the red */\\n    font-size: 16px; /* it will have no effect, since the inline style is higher */\\n  }\\n</style>"],[562,"The use of !important breaks the natural CSS cascade and makes the specificity of selectors useless. It makes the code harder to debug and to maintain, since such a style can only be overridden by another !important. You should use proper selectors and the order of declarations instead.","Why is !important bad practice?\\nCSS is based on the principle of the cascade and of selector specificity. When you use `!important` you ignore that mechanism entirely, which leads to chaos in your styles. Instead of working out why a style is not being applied, developers often add one more `!important`, creating an \\"arms race\\".\\nThe main problems\\n\\n- Breaking the cascade: `!important` overrides any rule, regardless of the specificity of the selector.\\n- Difficulty of debugging: to override a style with `!important` you have to use another `!important` with a higher specificity.\\n- Poor maintainability: the code becomes fragile — any change can break the styles in unexpected places.\\nAn example of bad practice\\n\\nThe right approach\\nUse selector specificity and the order of declarations. To override a component\'s style, for example, you can use a more specific selector or add a modifier class:\\n\\nSuch code is easy to read, to debug and to extend.\\nConclusion\\nAvoid `!important` in production code. Use it only for temporary solutions, or in user stylesheets, where you have no control over the source CSS. In every other case rely on specificity and on the order of the rules — that will make your CSS predictable and maintainable.",".button {\\n  background-color: blue !important;\\n}\\n\\n/* To override it you end up having to do this: */\\n.special .button {\\n  background-color: red !important;\\n}\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: blue;\\n}\\n\\n.button--primary {\\n  background-color: red;\\n}"],[563,"To get rid of !important you need to raise the specificity of your selector, rearrange the order in which the styles are linked in, or use more deeply nested selectors. You can also apply the BEM methodology in order to isolate styles, or CSS modules. It is important to reconsider your CSS architecture and to avoid conflicts through a proper organization of the code.","Understanding the problem with !important\\nThe !important directive in CSS breaks the natural cascade of styles, making a rule take priority regardless of specificity. This often leads to difficulties in maintaining and overriding styles. The main aim is to eliminate such dependencies and restore the predictability of the cascade.\\nThe ways of eliminating !important\\n\\n- Raising the selector\'s specificity: use more specific selectors — add a class to the parent element, for example, or use an ID. An example: instead of `.button { color: red !important; }` write `.container .button { color: red; }`.\\n- Changing the order in which the styles are linked in: if two rules have the same specificity, the last one wins. Move the rule you want further down in the file or link it in later.\\n- Using the BEM methodology: BEM (Block Element Modifier) creates unique class names, which rules conflicts out. For example: `.block__element--modifier { color: red; }`.\\n- CSS modules or CSS-in-JS: these approaches generate unique class names automatically, isolating the styles and removing the need for !important.\\nA practical example\\nSuppose there is a button with !important:\\n\\nThe solution is to remove the !important and raise the specificity:\\n\\nOr to use BEM:\\n\\nConclusion\\nEliminating !important improves the maintainability and predictability of CSS. Apply raised specificity, naming methodologies or style-isolation tools for clean, scalable code.",".btn { color: blue !important; }\\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* it will not take effect */\\n\\n.btn { color: blue; }\\n.special .btn { color: red; } /* now it works */\\n\\n.btn { color: blue; }\\n.btn--special { color: red; }"],[564,"The main CSS architectures are: BEM (Block-Element-Modifier), OOCSS (Object-Oriented CSS), SMACSS (Scalable and Modular Architecture for CSS) and ITCSS (Inverted Triangle CSS). BEM is popular thanks to its strict naming rules, which prevent style conflicts. OOCSS separates structure from presentation. SMACSS groups styles by category. ITCSS uses a hierarchy of specificity.","The main CSS architectures\\nA CSS architecture is an approach to organizing styles that makes the code predictable, reusable and easy to maintain. Without an architecture, CSS code quickly turns into chaos, with selector conflicts and difficulties in making changes.\\nBEM (Block-Element-Modifier)\\nBEM is the most popular methodology, developed at Yandex. It is based on strict rules for naming classes: the block, the element and the modifier. An example:\\n\\nBEM guarantees that selectors are unique and makes teamwork easier. Its disadvantage is long class names.\\nOOCSS (Object-Oriented CSS)\\nOOCSS separates structure from presentation. The class .media, for example, sets the structure (flexbox) while .media--dark sets the presentation. This improves reusability, but it can lead to a surfeit of classes in the HTML.\\nSMACSS (Scalable and Modular Architecture for CSS)\\nSMACSS groups styles by category: Base (the basic styles), Layout (the grid), Module (the components), State (the states) and Theme (the themes). It is a flexible approach that imposes no strict naming rules.\\nITCSS (Inverted Triangle CSS)\\nITCSS organizes styles by level of specificity: from the general (resets) to the specific (components). It is used with preprocessors and with BEM. It helps you keep control of the cascade and avoid !important.\\nConclusion\\nThe choice of architecture depends on the project. For large teams and long-lived projects BEM suits best, because of its strict rules and its predictability. For small projects you can use SMACSS or OOCSS. ITCSS is recommended for complex systems with preprocessors.",".button { }\\n.button__icon { }\\n.button--large { }"],[565,"The hardest layout elements are considered to be responsive and fluid layouts, complex grids (using CSS Grid, for example), animations and transitions, and cross-browser compatibility. Forms with non-standard controls and the layout of HTML emails also cause difficulty.","Difficult layout elements\\nIn modern web development some layout elements call for particular care because of their complexity. They include responsive layouts, which have to display correctly on devices with different screen resolutions. Media queries and relative units such as percentages, vw and vh are used for this.\\nComplex grids and animations\\nCreating complex grids — using CSS Grid or Flexbox, for example — can be a non-trivial task, especially when precise positioning of the elements is needed. Animations and transitions also call for a deep understanding of CSS properties such as transform, transition and animation, and of performance optimization.\\nAn example of the code: a responsive grid\\n\\nCross-browser compatibility and email layout\\nEnsuring that things display identically in every browser is another difficult task, especially for older versions of Internet Explorer. The layout of HTML emails is also considered difficult, because of the limited CSS support in mail clients, where you have to use table-based layout and inline styles.\\nConclusion: difficult layout elements call for a deep knowledge of CSS and an understanding of the principles of responsiveness and cross-browser compatibility. These skills are especially important when building modern web interfaces, where performance and uniformity of display matter.",".container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));\\n  gap: 16px;\\n}\\n\\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\\n  .container {\\n    grid-template-columns: 1fr;\\n  }\\n}"],[566,"For working with styles I have used the Sass and Less preprocessors, the BEM methodology for naming classes, CSS Modules for isolating styles in components, and CSS-in-JS libraries such as styled-components. These tools help you organize your code, avoid conflicts and speed development up.","Tools for working with styles\\nIn modern web development, styling goes beyond plain CSS. To improve the efficiency and maintainability of code, various tools and approaches are used. Let us look at the main ones.\\nPreprocessors\\nSass and Less are preprocessors that extend the capabilities of CSS. They add variables, nesting, mixins and functions, which makes code more modular and more convenient to reuse.\\n\\nMethodologies\\nBEM (Block Element Modifier) is a class-naming methodology that helps you structure your CSS and avoid conflicts. Every class describes an element\'s role: a block, an element or a modifier.\\n\\nCSS Modules\\nCSS Modules generate unique class names automatically, which prevents them from clashing between different components. This is especially useful in React applications.\\n\\nCSS-in-JS\\nLibraries such as styled-components let you write CSS right inside your JavaScript. That gives you the ability to use dynamic styles based on props and state.\\n\\nConclusion: the choice of tool depends on the project. For large projects with a team, Sass and BEM are suitable; for component architectures — CSS Modules or styled-components. The main thing is to keep your styles readable and scalable.","// An example in Sass\\n$primary-color: #3498db;\\n\\n.button {\\n  background-color: $primary-color;\\n  &:hover {\\n    background-color: darken($primary-color, 10%);\\n  }\\n}\\n\\n<div class=\\"card card--featured\\">\\n  <h2 class=\\"card__title\\">A heading</h2>\\n  <p class=\\"card__text\\">Some text</p>\\n</div>\\n\\n// styles.module.css\\n.button {\\n  background: blue;\\n}\\n\\n// Component.jsx\\nimport styles from \'./styles.module.css\';\\n<button className={styles.button}>Click</button>\\n\\nimport styled from \'styled-components\';\\n\\nconst Button = styled.button`\\n  background: ${props => props.primary ? \'blue\' : \'gray\'};\\n  color: white;\\n`;"],[567,"To lay block elements out horizontally you can use CSS Flexbox, by giving the parent container display: flex, or the inline-block property, by giving the elements display: inline-block. Flexbox is more flexible and more convenient for responsive layouts, while inline-block is suited to simple cases.","Laying block elements out horizontally in CSS\\nTo place several block elements on a single line, two main approaches are used: Flexbox and inline-block. Flexbox is the modern method, which gives you flexible control over placement, alignment and the distribution of space between the elements. Inline-block is the older way, in which the elements behave as inline ones while keeping their block properties (width, height, spacing).\\nFlexbox\\nThe parent container is given `display: flex`, and every child element lines up in a row automatically. Additional properties such as `justify-content` and `align-items` let you control the alignment.\\n\\nInline-block\\nEach element is given `display: inline-block`. It is important to bear in mind that a gap can appear between such elements because of the line breaks in the HTML. To get rid of it you can give the parent `font-size: 0` or use comments.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFlexbox is preferable for complex responsive layouts, since it is simpler to control and has no problems with gaps. Inline-block is suited to simple cases, where you need to put elements on a line quickly without any additional styles.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: space-between; /* the distribution of the items */\\n}\\n.item {\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n  background: lightblue;\\n}\\n\\n.item {\\n  display: inline-block;\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 50px;\\n  background: lightcoral;\\n  vertical-align: top; /* alignment to the top edge */\\n}"],[568,"For sorting, radio buttons are normally used, since the user can choose only one sort option (by price or by date, for example). Checkboxes are suited to filtering, where several values can be chosen at once. Using radio buttons makes the interface intuitive and prevents confusion.","The difference between checkboxes and radio buttons\\nCheckboxes and radio buttons are form controls that let the user make a choice. The main difference is that checkboxes are intended for multiple choice (you can tick several items) while radio buttons are for a single choice (only one option from a group).\\nTheir use in sorting\\nSorting means ordering data by one criterion at a time. The user can sort products by price (ascending or descending) or by the date they were added, for example. It is impossible to sort by price and by date at the same time — that is logically contradictory. For sorting, therefore, radio buttons are ideal, since they make it plain that only one option can be chosen.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to use checkboxes\\nCheckboxes are appropriate in filters, where several values can be chosen at once — a filter by product category (electronics, clothing, books), for example. In that case the user can tick several categories, and the system will show the products from all of the chosen categories.\\nConclusion: for sorting, always use radio buttons, in order to avoid ambiguity and make the interface intuitive. Leave checkboxes for filtering, where multiple choice is needed.","<!-- Wrong: checkboxes for sorting -->\\n<label><input type=\\"checkbox\\" name=\\"sort\\" value=\\"price\\"> By price</label>\\n<label><input type=\\"checkbox\\" name=\\"sort\\" value=\\"date\\"> By date</label>\\n\\n<!-- Right: radio buttons for sorting -->\\n<label><input type=\\"radio\\" name=\\"sort\\" value=\\"price\\"> By price</label>\\n<label><input type=\\"radio\\" name=\\"sort\\" value=\\"date\\"> By date</label>"],[569,"A toggle is better for turning a feature on or off instantly without submitting a form — for a dark theme, for example. A checkbox is suited to choosing options in a form, where the action needs confirming — when agreeing to terms, for example. A toggle shows the current state visually, while a checkbox shows a choice from a list.","The difference between a toggle and a checkbox\\nA toggle and a checkbox both let you control a binary state, but their use depends on the context and on the behaviour the user expects. A toggle imitates a physical switch and is normally used for applying a change immediately — turning notifications on or changing the theme, for example. A checkbox, by contrast, is more often found in forms, where the choice is only recorded once the data is submitted — when choosing options in your profile settings, for example.\\nWhen to use a toggle\\nA toggle is suited to actions that take effect straight away without any additional confirmation — switching \\"Do not disturb\\" mode on a phone, for example. Visually a toggle shows the current state (on/off) and is often accompanied by an animation.\\n\\nWhen to use a checkbox\\nA checkbox is better used in forms where explicit confirmation is needed — when agreeing to terms of use or choosing several options, for example. A checkbox causes no immediate changes but holds its state until the form is submitted.\\n\\nConclusion\\nUse a toggle for instant actions with no confirmation, and a checkbox for choices in forms that are submitted afterwards. The right choice improves the user experience and makes the interface more intuitive.","<label>\\n  <input type=\\"checkbox\\" role=\\"switch\\" /> Dark theme\\n</label>\\n\\n<form>\\n  <label>\\n    <input type=\\"checkbox\\" name=\\"agree\\" /> I agree to the terms\\n  </label>\\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button>\\n</form>"],[570,"From a UX point of view it is better to use a separate visible label rather than a placeholder. A placeholder disappears as soon as text is entered, which forces the user to remember the hint. A label is always visible, improves accessibility for screen readers and conforms to the WCAG standards. A placeholder can be used as an additional hint, but not as a replacement for a label.","Why is a label better than a placeholder?\\nPlaceholder text is text inside an input field that disappears as soon as the user starts typing. That creates several problems from a UX point of view:\\n\\n- The user loses the context once they start typing, especially if the form is long or calls for precise data.\\n- A placeholder often has low contrast, which makes it hard for people with poor vision to read.\\n- Screen readers do not always read a placeholder out correctly, which breaks accessibility.\\nA label is a separate piece of text above the field or beside it, which is always visible. It does not disappear as you type, which helps the user remember what they have to enter. A label also improves clickability: clicking the label moves focus to the input field.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen should a placeholder be used?\\nA placeholder can be used as an additional hint alongside a label — to indicate the format of the data (a date, a telephone number), for example. But never use one in place of a label. That is especially important for registration, payment or personal-data forms.\\nConclusion: for the best UX, always use a visible label. A placeholder is only an auxiliary element, which must not be the only source of information about a field.","<!-- Bad: a placeholder alone -->\\n<input type=\\"email\\" placeholder=\\"Enter your email\\">\\n\\n<!-- Good: a label + a placeholder as a hint -->\\n<label for=\\"email\\">Email</label>\\n<input type=\\"email\\" id=\\"email\\" placeholder=\\"example@mail.com\\">"],[571,"The main action button (the CTA) in a mobile interface is better placed in the lower part of the screen, closer to the edge, rather than in the centre. This has to do with the thumb\'s reach zone when the phone is held in one hand. The centre of the screen is often an awkward zone, requiring the thumb to overstretch or the grip to change.","Why the button\'s placement matters\\nIn mobile interfaces the user most often interacts with the screen with one hand, holding the device and using their thumb. According to Fitts\'s law, the time it takes to reach a target depends on the distance to it and on the size of the target. Placing the main action button (the Call to Action, CTA) in the centre of the screen forces the user to stretch their thumb, which increases both the time and the likelihood of a mistake.\\nThe thumb\'s reach zones\\nResearch shows that the most comfortable zone for tapping is the lower third of the screen, especially near the edges. The centre of the screen and the upper part require either an overstretched thumb or a change of grip, which is awkward. Buttons such as \\"Buy\\", \\"Send\\" and \\"Next\\" are therefore placed at the bottom.\\nA practical example\\nConsider an order form in a mobile application. The \\"Place order\\" button should be pinned to the bottom of the screen rather than floating in the centre:\\n\\nThe exceptions\\nIn some cases — in games, or when two hands are used — the button can be in the centre. For typical mobile applications (e-commerce, social networks, banking), however, placement at the bottom is the standard.\\nConclusion: the main action button in a mobile interface should be placed in the lower part of the screen, in order to minimize the user\'s effort and speed the interaction up. This improves usability and conversion.","<div class=\\"order-form\\">\\n  <!-- the form fields -->\\n  <div class=\\"sticky-bottom\\">\\n    <button class=\\"btn-primary\\">Place order</button>\\n  </div>\\n</div>\\n\\n<style>\\n.sticky-bottom {\\n  position: fixed;\\n  bottom: 0;\\n  left: 0;\\n  right: 0;\\n  padding: 12px;\\n  background: white;\\n  box-shadow: 0 -2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);\\n}\\n.btn-primary {\\n  width: 100%;\\n  padding: 16px;\\n  font-size: 18px;\\n}\\n</style>"],[572,"To choose a numeric value from a large range it is better to use `input type=\\"range\\"` (a slider) with an additional numeric field. A select with a great many options is awkward when there are a lot of them, since it requires scrolling and makes searching harder. A range gives a quick visual choice, while the numeric field gives an exact value.","Choosing a UI control for numeric input\\nWhen building forms, the task of choosing a numeric value from a range often arises — an age, a price or a quantity, for example. Using a `<select>` element with a large number of options (more than 10–15) makes the user experience worse: the user has to scroll a long list and hunt for the value they want, which slows the interaction down. The alternative is a combination of `<input type=\\"range\\">` and `<input type=\\"number\\">`.\\nWhy range + input is better\\n\\n- Convenience for large ranges: a slider lets you move across the whole range quickly, judging your position visually.\\n- Precision: a numeric field beside the slider gives you the ability to enter an exact value where necessary.\\n- Responsiveness: a range takes up little room and works well on mobile devices.\\n- Accessibility: the keyboard can be used for fine adjustment (the arrows, Page Up/Down).\\nAn example implementation\\n\\nWhen to use a select\\nA `<select>` element is justified when the number of options is small (up to 10–15) or when the values have meaningful names (choosing a month, for example). For numeric ranges with a large number of steps a select becomes awkward.\\nConclusion: to choose a numeric value from a large range, use a combination of a range and a numeric field — it improves the UX, speeds input up and preserves precision. Leave the select for short lists with named options.","<label for=\\"price\\">Price:</label>\\n<input type=\\"range\\" id=\\"price\\" min=\\"0\\" max=\\"10000\\" step=\\"100\\" value=\\"5000\\">\\n<input type=\\"number\\" id=\\"priceValue\\" min=\\"0\\" max=\\"10000\\" step=\\"100\\" value=\\"5000\\">\\n<script>\\n  const range = document.getElementById(\'price\');\\n  const number = document.getElementById(\'priceValue\');\\n  range.addEventListener(\'input\', () => number.value = range.value);\\n  number.addEventListener(\'input\', () => range.value = number.value);\\n<\/script>"],[573,"If the combined width of the child elements exceeds the width of the container, then by default (flex-shrink: 1) the elements shrink. If one element has flex-shrink: 0, it will not shrink and its width will stay unchanged. The remaining elements will shrink in order to fit in the container, but if that shrinking is not enough, the element with flex-shrink: 0 can cause the container to overflow.","How flex-shrink works in CSS Flexbox\\nThe `flex-shrink` property determines how much a flex item can shrink if the combined width of every item exceeds the width of the container. The default value is 1, which lets the items shrink proportionally. If you set `flex-shrink: 0`, the item will not shrink and will keep its original width.\\nAn example of the behaviour\\nConsider a container 300px wide with three child elements of 150px each. Their combined width is 450px, which is 150px more than the container. If one element has `flex-shrink: 0`, it will stay 150px and the other two will shrink in order to compensate for the overflow. If that shrinking is not enough, the element with `flex-shrink: 0` can extend beyond the container\'s bounds.\\n\\nPractical use\\nThis property is useful when you need to fix the size of a particular element — an icon or a button, for example — so that it does not shrink as the container\'s width changes. The remaining elements will adapt, taking up the room that is left.\\nConclusion: use `flex-shrink: 0` when an element has to keep its width regardless of the container overflowing, and let the other elements shrink.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  width: 300px;\\n}\\n.item {\\n  width: 150px;\\n}\\n.item.no-shrink {\\n  flex-shrink: 0;\\n}"],[574,"For elements with position: absolute the containing block is the nearest ancestor whose position is other than static (relative, absolute, fixed or sticky, for example). If there is no such ancestor, the containing block becomes the root element (html). position: relative on the parent is one of the options, but not the only one.","How the containing block is determined for position: absolute\\nAn element with `position: absolute` is positioned relative to its containing block. The containing block is the nearest ancestor whose `position` property is set to a value other than `static`. That can be `relative`, `absolute`, `fixed` or `sticky`. If there is no such ancestor, the containing block becomes the root `<html>` element.\\nExamples\\nConsider several situations:\\n\\n- A parent with `position: relative` — the absolutely positioned child will be positioned relative to it.\\n- A parent with `position: absolute` — it can likewise be the containing block for a nested absolute element.\\n- A parent with `position: fixed` — the same.\\n- If no ancestor has an explicitly set `position` (other than `static`), the containing block will be `<html>`.\\nA practical example of the code\\n\\nIn this example the red block will sit 10px from the top and the left of its blue parent, since the parent has `position: relative`.\\nConclusion\\nTo position an element with `position: absolute`, one of its ancestors has to be given an explicit `position` (not `static`). That lets you control the placement of elements on a page flexibly, without any dependence on the root element.","<div style=\\"position: relative; width: 300px; height: 200px; background: lightblue;\\">\\n  <div style=\\"position: absolute; top: 10px; left: 10px; background: red;\\">\\n    An absolute block\\n  </div>\\n</div>"],[575,"The main types of visualization are: bar charts for comparing categories, line charts for trends over time, pie charts for shares, scatter plots for correlation, histograms for distribution, heatmaps for data matrices. The choice depends on the type of data and on the aim of the analysis.","The main types of data visualization\\nData visualization is the graphical presentation of information, which helps you spot patterns, trends and anomalies quickly. Choosing the right type of chart is critically important to interpreting the data correctly.\\nBar and line charts\\nBar charts are ideal for comparing values across categories — sales by month, for example. Line charts show change over time and trends — the growth in users, for example.\\nPie and scatter charts\\nPie charts show the shares of a whole, but they are not recommended when there are a great many categories. Scatter plots show the relationship between two variables and help you spot correlation.\\nHistograms and heatmaps\\nHistograms show the distribution of data — the age of your users, for example. Heatmaps visualize data matrices and are often used to analyse correlations or activity over time.\\nAn example of the code in Python with the Matplotlib library\\n\\nConclusion\\nChoosing the right visualization makes data analysis easier and helps you get your conclusions across to your audience. Use bar and line charts for trends and comparisons, pie charts only for a small number of categories, and scatter plots for finding relationships.","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\n\\n# A line chart\\nx = [1, 2, 3, 4]\\ny = [10, 20, 15, 25]\\nplt.plot(x, y)\\nplt.title(\'The sales trend\')\\nplt.show()\\n\\n# A bar chart\\ncategories = [\'A\', \'B\', \'C\']\\nvalues = [5, 7, 3]\\nplt.bar(categories, values)\\nplt.show()"],[576,"Use auto-fit or auto-fill together with minmax() in grid-template-columns. For example: grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(250px, 1fr)). This makes the browser choose the number of columns automatically, adjusting to the width of the container. The difference between auto-fit and auto-fill is that auto-fit collapses the empty tracks while auto-fill keeps them.","Dynamic columns in CSS Grid\\nInstead of a fixed number of columns — `grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr`, for example — you can use a combination of `repeat()`, `auto-fit`/`auto-fill` and `minmax()`. This lets the grid determine the number of columns automatically according to the space available.\\nHow it works\\nThe `repeat()` function takes two arguments: the number of repetitions and the size of the track. If you give `auto-fit` or `auto-fill` instead of a number, the browser computes how many columns will fit on the basis of the minimum width given in `minmax()`.\\n\\nIn this example each column will be at least 200px, but it can stretch (1fr) if there is free room. The number of columns will change as the container\'s width changes.\\nThe difference between auto-fit and auto-fill\\n\\n- auto-fill — creates as many columns as fit, even if some of them are empty (they take up room but contain no items).\\n- auto-fit — collapses the empty columns, letting the items take up all of the available space.\\nAn example with auto-fill:\\n\\nIf the container is 500px wide and the minimum column width is 150px, then 3 columns will fit (3*150=450) and there will be room left for a fourth, but it will be empty. With auto-fit the fourth column will vanish and the three columns will stretch across the whole width.\\nPractical use\\nThis approach is ideal for product cards, image galleries or any list where the number of items is not known in advance. It saves you from writing media queries for every breakpoint.\\nConclusion: use `repeat(auto-fit, minmax(…))` for responsive grids without media queries. It makes layout flexible and maintainable.",".container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px, 1fr));\\n  gap: 16px;\\n}\\n\\n.container {\\n  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(150px, 1fr));\\n}"],[577,"eCharts provides a wide range of chart types (line, bar, pie, maps and others) and supports animation, interactivity and responsiveness. In addition you can implement data filters, conditional formatting (changing colour according to the values, for example), custom legends and tooltips, and integration with other libraries for processing data.","eCharts\'s main capabilities\\neCharts is a powerful JavaScript library for data visualization, developed by Baidu. It supports a great many chart types: line, bar, pie, scatter, radar, maps (geographical ones included), and complex combined charts as well. The library gives high performance when working with large volumes of data and supports animated transitions and interactive elements such as zooming, panning and highlighting data.\\nAdditional functionality\\nBesides its built-in capabilities, eCharts lets you implement:\\n\\n- Data filters: through custom controls (dropdown lists or sliders, for example) you can change the data displayed dynamically, using the API to update the chart\'s options.\\n- Conditional formatting: configuring the colour, size or style of elements according to the data values (through the visual channels in the series, for example).\\n- Custom tooltips and legends: you can create tooltips of your own with HTML content, or interactive legends for filtering the series.\\n- Integration with other libraries: with D3.js for additional visual effects, for example, or with Lodash for processing data.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nConclusion\\neCharts is a flexible tool for creating interactive visualizations, one that is easy to extend with custom filters and conditional formatting. It is worth applying in projects that call for fast, intuitive work with data, especially where geographical maps or large volumes of information are needed.","// Initializing the chart\\nvar myChart = echarts.init(document.getElementById(\'main\'));\\n\\n// The options, with conditional formatting\\nvar option = {\\n  xAxis: { type: \'category\', data: [\'A\', \'B\', \'C\'] },\\n  yAxis: { type: \'value\' },\\n  series: [{\\n    type: \'bar\',\\n    data: [\\n      { value: 10, itemStyle: { color: \'green\' } },\\n      { value: 20, itemStyle: { color: \'orange\' } },\\n      { value: 30, itemStyle: { color: \'red\' } }\\n    ]\\n  }]\\n};\\n\\n// Applying a filter through a button\\ndocument.getElementById(\'filterBtn\').onclick = function() {\\n  var filteredData = [10, 20]; // an example of filtering\\n  myChart.setOption({ series: [{ data: filteredData }] });\\n};\\n\\nmyChart.setOption(option);"],[578,"In Flexbox the alignment is governed by properties on the container and on the items. justify-content aligns along the main axis (horizontally, if flex-direction: row). align-items aligns along the cross axis (vertically). align-self overrides the alignment for a particular item. There is also align-content for multi-line containers.","The main alignment properties in Flexbox\\nFlexbox provides powerful tools for aligning items inside a container. The main axis is determined by the `flex-direction` property, and the cross axis is perpendicular to it.\\nThe properties for the container\\n\\n- `justify-content` — alignment along the main axis. The values: `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `center`, `space-between`, `space-around`, `space-evenly`.\\n- `align-items` — alignment along the cross axis for every item. The values: `stretch`, `flex-start`, `flex-end`, `center`, `baseline`.\\n- `align-content` — alignment of the lines when wrapping (it requires `flex-wrap: wrap`).\\nThe properties for the items\\n\\n- `align-self` — it overrides `align-items` for a particular item.\\n- `margin: auto` — it can be used to push an item to an edge.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nIn this example every item is aligned to the centre of the container, but the item with the class `special` will be pushed to the bottom edge.\\nConclusion\\nFlexbox is ideal for one-dimensional layouts (rows or columns), where simple, predictable alignment is needed. Use it for centring, for distributing space and for building responsive interfaces without complicated calculations.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: center; /* centred horizontally */\\n  align-items: center;     /* centred vertically */\\n  height: 200px;\\n}\\n\\n.item {\\n  width: 100px;\\n  height: 100px;\\n}\\n\\n.item.special {\\n  align-self: flex-end; /* this item will be at the bottom */\\n}"],[579,"Flexbox is intended for one-dimensional layout (in a row or in a column), whereas Grid is for two-dimensional layout (rows and columns at the same time). Flexbox governs the distribution of items along a single axis; Grid lets you position items precisely in the cells of a table. Flexbox suits components and small blocks better, Grid whole pages and complex grids.","The main difference\\nFlexbox (display: flex) and Grid (display: grid) are two powerful CSS tools for building layout. The main difference is that Flexbox works in one dimension (either a row or a column) whereas Grid works in two (rows and columns at the same time).\\nWhen to use Flexbox\\nFlexbox is ideal for distributing items inside a container along one axis. It is excellent for:\\n\\n- Centring an element vertically and horizontally\\n- Creating flexible rows of menu items or navigation\\n- Aligning cards of different heights in a row\\n\\nWhen to use Grid\\nGrid is intended for building complex two-dimensional grids, where you need to control the position of the items by row and by column at the same time. Examples:\\n\\n- A page layout (header, sidebar, content, footer)\\n- An image gallery with cells of different sizes\\n- Tabular data, where the structure matters\\n\\nConclusion\\nFlexbox is better applied to components and one-dimensional layouts, Grid to two-dimensional page layouts. These technologies are often combined: Grid sets the overall structure while Flexbox governs the items inside the cells.",".container {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: space-between;\\n  align-items: center;\\n}\\n\\n.item {\\n  flex: 1;\\n  margin: 10px;\\n}\\n\\n.grid-container {\\n  display: grid;\\n  grid-template-columns: 1fr 2fr 1fr;\\n  grid-template-rows: auto 1fr auto;\\n  gap: 20px;\\n}\\n\\n.header {\\n  grid-column: 1 / -1;\\n}"],[580,"A Reflow (Layout) is caused by properties that change an element\'s geometry: width, height, margin, padding, border, position, display, font-size. A Repaint is caused by properties that change only the appearance: color, background-color, visibility, outline, box-shadow (without any change of size). A Reflow is more expensive, since it requires the positions of every child and neighbouring element to be recomputed.","The difference between a Repaint and a Reflow\\nWhen the browser paints a page it goes through several stages: building the DOM, the CSSOM and the render tree, computing the geometry (Layout/Reflow) and painting the pixels (Paint/Repaint). Some CSS properties touch only an element\'s appearance without changing its size or position — they cause only a Repaint. Others change the geometry, which leads to Layout being recomputed (a Reflow) and a subsequent Repaint.\\nProperties that cause only a Repaint\\n\\n- color\\n- background-color, background-image (without any change of size)\\n- visibility\\n- outline (without any change of size)\\n- box-shadow (without a blur that affects the size)\\n- text-decoration\\n- border-color\\nProperties that cause a Reflow (Layout)\\n\\n- width, height\\n- margin, padding\\n- border-width\\n- position (top, left, right, bottom)\\n- display (none/block and so on)\\n- font-size, font-family\\n- line-height\\n- overflow\\n- float, clear\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nPractical use\\nTo optimize the performance of animations and of frequent style changes (in games or drag-and-drop, for example), you should avoid the properties that cause a Reflow. Use transform and opacity — they are handled at the compositing level and cause neither a Reflow nor a Repaint.\\nConclusion: knowing this difference helps you write fast interfaces, especially when working with animations and dynamic changes to the DOM. For maximum performance, give preference to transform and opacity.","// It causes only a Repaint\\nelement.style.color = \'red\';\\nelement.style.backgroundColor = \'blue\';\\n\\n// It causes a Reflow + a Repaint\\nelement.style.width = \'200px\';\\nelement.style.height = \'100px\';\\nelement.style.margin = \'10px\';"],[581,"An inline style is a way of setting CSS styles right in an HTML element through the style attribute. It applies only to that particular element and has the highest priority among ordinary styles. It is used for quick one-off fixes, but it makes code harder to maintain.","What is an inline style?\\nInline styles are CSS rules written directly in an HTML tag by means of the `style` attribute. For example: `<div style=\\"color: red;\\">Text</div>`. Such styles apply only to one element and are not reused.\\nHow does it work?\\nThe browser handles inline styles as ordinary CSS declarations, but with a very high priority. They override styles from external files and from the `<style>` tag, unless `!important` is used. This follows from the rules of specificity: an inline style has a specificity of 1-0-0-0, which is higher than that of class or id selectors.\\nAn example of its use\\n\\nWhen should it be used?\\nInline styles are convenient for quick experiments, for changing styles dynamically through JavaScript (`element.style.color = \'red\'`, for example) or in HTML emails, where external CSS is not supported. For large projects, however, it is better to use classes and external stylesheets — that makes maintenance and reuse easier.\\nConclusion\\nAn inline style is a simple, fast way of giving an element a style, but it reduces the readability and the scalability of the code. Use it selectively, and prefer CSS classes for systematic solutions.","<!-- HTML -->\\n<p style=\\"font-size: 18px; color: #333;\\">Hello, world!</p>\\n\\n<!-- The equivalent in CSS -->\\n<style>\\n  p {\\n    font-size: 18px;\\n    color: #333;\\n  }\\n</style>"],[582,"The simplest way is to use flexbox: give the parent display: flex, align-items: center and justify-content: center. You can also use CSS Grid with place-items: center, or absolute positioning with transform: translate(-50%, -50%). For centring text horizontally, text-align: center will do; for a block — margin: 0 auto.","The main approaches to centring\\nCentring elements is one of the most frequent tasks in layout. Depending on the context (text, a block element, an element of unknown size) different methods are used. Let us look at the most popular and most reliable of them.\\nFlexbox — the universal solution\\nFlexbox lets you centre an element on both axes with just two properties. It works for any number of child elements and does not require you to know their dimensions.\\n\\nIf you need to centre just one element, you can use margin: auto inside the flex container.\\nCSS Grid — the modern alternative\\nGrid gives the same result by means of the place-items property, which combines align-items and justify-items.\\n\\nAbsolute positioning\\nIf the parent has a set height, you can use position: absolute and transform. This method works even for elements of unknown size.\\n\\nCentring text and a block horizontally\\nFor text inside a block, text-align: center is enough. For a block element with a fixed width — margin: 0 auto.\\n\\nConclusion\\nFor most modern projects choose flexbox or grid — they are simpler and responsive. Absolute positioning is useful for modal windows or overlays, where an element has to be laid precisely over another. Knowing every method helps you solve layout problems quickly, whatever the situation.",".parent {\\n  display: flex;\\n  justify-content: center; /* horizontally */\\n  align-items: center;     /* vertically */\\n}\\n\\n.parent {\\n  display: grid;\\n  place-items: center;\\n}\\n\\n.parent {\\n  position: relative;\\n}\\n.child {\\n  position: absolute;\\n  top: 50%;\\n  left: 50%;\\n  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\\n}\\n\\n.text { text-align: center; }\\n.block {\\n  width: 200px;\\n  margin: 0 auto;\\n}"],[583,"!important in CSS raises the priority of a particular CSS property, overriding the usual rules of the cascade and of specificity. It is used when a style absolutely has to be applied — to override styles from libraries or user stylesheets, for example. Overusing !important does, however, make code harder to maintain, so it should be used rarely and deliberately.","What is !important?\\nCSS works by a cascade: the browser chooses which rule to apply on the basis of the specificity of the selectors and the order of declaration. Sometimes, however, a style has to be overridden come what may, even if it is set with a greater specificity or later in the code. For that there is the keyword `!important`, which is added after a property\'s value and raises its priority above all of the ordinary rules.\\nHow does it work?\\nWhen the browser encounters `!important`, it first sorts every declaration by importance: the ordinary ones come first, then the important ones. Within each group the usual rules of specificity and order apply. That means `!important` overrides even inline styles, unless they too are marked as important.\\n\\nWhen should it be used?\\nMost often `!important` is used to override styles from third-party libraries when you have no access to their source code, or for temporary fixes. It is also useful for styles that have to be immutable — for critically important interface elements, for example. Using `!important` systematically does, however, make CSS hard to maintain, since it breaks the natural cascade and makes debugging harder.\\nConclusion\\nUse `!important` only in extreme cases, when other ways of overriding styles are unavailable or too complicated. In the long run it is better to rely on a proper organization of your CSS and on the specificity of your selectors, so that the code stays clean and predictable.","/* An ordinary rule */\\n.button { background-color: blue; }\\n\\n/* Overriding it with !important */\\n.button { background-color: red !important; }\\n\\n/* An inline style will not beat !important */\\n<button class=\\"button\\" style=\\"background-color: green\\">A button</button>\\n// The end result is that the button will be red"],[584,"For a simple shift it is better to use transform: translate, since it does not affect the document flow and runs on the GPU, which improves performance. position: relative shifts an element visually but leaves its place in the layout, which can cause recalculations. transform also causes no reflow or repaint, only compositing.","The main difference\\nThe `position: relative` property shifts an element relative to its normal position, while its original place in the document flow is preserved. That means the other elements do not fill the space that has been freed up, and the browser has to recompute the layout when the position changes. The `transform: translate` property also shifts an element visually, but it does not affect the document flow and does not trigger a layout recalculation. Instead the browser creates a separate layer for the element and moves it on the GPU, which is considerably faster.\\nPerformance\\nWith `position: relative`, changing the coordinates causes a reflow (a recomputation of the geometry) and a repaint, which can be costly, especially in animations. `transform: translate` works at the compositing stage, which is performed on the GPU, so animations using it are usually smooth and do not load the main thread. This is especially important on mobile devices and in complex interfaces.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nWhen to use position: relative\\n`position: relative` is useful when you need to shift an element while keeping its place in the flow — for overlapping, or for creating a positioning context for absolutely positioned child elements. It is also used to create a z-index context.\\nConclusion\\nFor a simple shift, and especially for animations, `transform: translate` is preferable, because of its better performance and the absence of any effect on the layout. `position: relative` is worth applying only when the element\'s place has to be preserved or a positioning context created.","/* Bad for animation */\\n.element {\\n  position: relative;\\n  left: 100px;\\n  top: 50px;\\n}\\n\\n/* Good for animation */\\n.element {\\n  transform: translate(100px, 50px);\\n}"],[585,"The position property determines how an element is positioned on a page. The main values: static (the default), relative (an offset from its own place), absolute (positioning relative to the nearest positioned ancestor), fixed (relative to the browser window) and sticky (a combination of relative and fixed). Each value affects the document flow and the element\'s behaviour as the page scrolls.","What is CSS position?\\nThe `position` property in CSS governs how an element is placed in a document. By default every element has the value `static`, which means the ordinary document flow. By changing this property you can take elements out of the flow, offset them or pin them to the screen.\\nThe main values\\n\\n- static — the standard behaviour; the element follows the document flow, and the `top`, `right`, `bottom`, `left` and `z-index` properties have no effect.\\n- relative — the element stays in the flow, but it can be offset from its original place by means of those properties. The neighbouring elements do not move.\\n- absolute — the element is removed from the flow entirely and positioned relative to its nearest ancestor whose `position` is not `static`. If there is no such ancestor, then relative to `html`.\\n- fixed — the element is removed from the flow and positioned relative to the browser window; it stays where it is as the page scrolls.\\n- sticky — the element behaves like `relative` until it reaches the given scroll threshold, after which it \\"sticks\\" and behaves like `fixed`.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHow this is used in practice\\nA knowledge of `position` is necessary for creating modal windows, dropdown menus, fixed headers, tooltips and complex layouts. `absolute`, for example, is often used to lay elements over one another, and `sticky` to pin table headers as the page scrolls.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding every value of `position` is a basic competence for a frontend developer. Use `relative` for fine adjustment, `absolute` for positioning inside a container, `fixed` for permanent interface elements and `sticky` for elements that must stay visible as the page scrolls.",".box {\\n  position: relative;\\n  top: 20px;\\n  left: 30px;\\n}\\n\\n.modal {\\n  position: fixed;\\n  top: 50%;\\n  left: 50%;\\n  transform: translate(-50%, -50%);\\n}\\n\\n.header {\\n  position: sticky;\\n  top: 0;\\n}"],[586,"BEM (Block, Element, Modifier) is a methodology for naming CSS classes and organizing code. It helps you create reusable components, makes maintenance easier and prevents style conflicts. Classes are written in the pattern block__element_modifier, which makes the structure clear and predictable.","What is BEM?\\nBEM (Block, Element, Modifier) is an interface-development methodology proposed by Yandex. It offers strict rules for naming CSS classes and structuring code, in order to make frontend projects scalable and easy to maintain. The basic idea is to break an interface down into independent blocks that can be reused in different places.\\nThe main concepts\\n\\n- A block — a self-contained entity: a button, a menu or a form, for example. A block\'s name is a single word without underscores: `button`, `menu`.\\n- An element — a part of a block that has no meaning outside it. An element\'s name is appended with a double underscore: `button__icon`, `menu__item`.\\n- A modifier — a state or variant of a block/element. It is appended with a single underscore: `button_disabled`, `menu__item_active`.\\nAn example of the code\\n\\nHere `card` is the block, `card__image` and `card__title` are elements, and `card__button_primary` is a modifier that sets the button\'s style.\\nWhy is it needed?\\nBEM solves several problems: name conflicts (the classes are unique), code reuse (the blocks are independent) and readability (the class name tells you at once what something is and where it sits). This is especially useful in large projects with team development, where it matters to keep to a single style.\\nConclusion\\nBEM is worth applying in projects where long-term maintenance and scalability matter. It needs no additional tools and works with any preprocessor or framework, making CSS code predictable and structured.","<div class=\\"card\\">\\n  <img class=\\"card__image\\" src=\\"...\\" alt=\\"...\\">\\n  <h3 class=\\"card__title\\">A heading</h3>\\n  <p class=\\"card__text\\">A description</p>\\n  <button class=\\"card__button card__button_primary\\">Buy</button>\\n</div>"],[587,"BEM focuses on a strict structure of class names through blocks, elements and modifiers, which makes code predictable and reusable. SMACSS divides styles into categories (base, layout, modules and so on), while OOCSS emphasizes the separation of structure from appearance and reuse through composition. BEM is stricter in its syntax, whereas SMACSS and OOCSS give more freedom in how things are organized.","An introduction to CSS methodologies\\nCSS class-naming methodologies help you organize styles in large projects, improving readability, reusability and maintainability. BEM, SMACSS and OOCSS are three popular approaches, each with its own principles and emphases.\\nThe main differences\\nBEM (Block, Element, Modifier) offers a rigid naming scheme: a block is an independent component, an element is a part of a block, and a modifier is a variant of state or appearance. For example, `button` is a block, `button__icon` an element and `button--primary` a modifier. This makes the structure predictable and easy to scale.\\nSMACSS (Scalable and Modular Architecture for CSS) classifies styles into five categories: base, layout, modules, states and themes. It imposes no strict naming rules but offers an organization of files and selectors by purpose. Classes for layout, for example, can have the prefix `l-`, and those for states `is-`.\\nOOCSS (Object-Oriented CSS) focuses on separating structure from appearance and on reuse through composition. It encourages the creation of universal classes that can be combined — `btn` for a basic button and `btn-large` for the size, for example.\\nA practical example\\n\\nWhen to use which\\nBEM is ideal for large projects with a clear component architecture, where a strict structure matters. SMACSS suits projects where styles need to be organized flexibly by layer. OOCSS is useful when maximum reuse and minimal code duplication are needed.\\nConclusion: the choice of methodology depends on the size of the project, on the team and on preference. BEM gives strictness and predictability, SMACSS flexibility of organization, OOCSS reusability. They are often combined — using BEM for naming and SMACSS for the file structure, for example.","// BEM\\n.button { /* a block */ }\\n.button__icon { /* an element */ }\\n.button--primary { /* a modifier */ }\\n\\n// SMACSS\\n.l-header { /* layout */ }\\n.btn { /* a module */ }\\n.is-active { /* a state */ }\\n\\n// OOCSS\\n.btn { /* structure */ }\\n.btn-large { /* appearance */ }"],[588,"Semantic variables separate the meaning from the specific value, which makes styles easier to maintain and to override. Instead of tying things to the h1 tag you use a variable that can be overridden in any context. This makes the code more flexible, reduces duplication and makes creating themes easier.","The problem with styling tags directly\\nWhen you set styles directly on h1 or h2 in your global CSS, you tie the appearance rigidly to a particular element. That creates problems: if you need to change a heading in a particular block, you end up having to override the styles with a higher specificity or to use !important. In time the CSS grows, conflicts appear, and maintenance becomes difficult.\\nWhat semantic variables are\\nSemantic variables (or design tokens) are named values that describe a style\'s purpose rather than its implementation. The variable --font-heading-h1, for example, may hold a font size, but its name says that this is the font for the main heading. You can use it in any component without tying yourself to a tag.\\n\\nThe advantages of the approach\\n\\n- Flexibility: the same style can be applied to different elements.\\n- Overriding: in a particular component you can change the variable without touching the global styles.\\n- Themes: it is easy to create a light and a dark theme by changing the values of the variables.\\n- Consistency: every heading uses a single source of truth.\\nA practical example\\nImagine you have a card where the heading has to be smaller than a standard h1. Instead of overriding h1 you create a local variable:\\n\\nThis works without conflicts and keeps the code readable.\\nConclusion\\nSemantic variables are worth applying in any project where scalability and maintenance matter. They are especially useful in design systems and when working with themes, since they separate the visual decisions from the HTML structure.",":root {\\n  --font-heading-h1: 2.5rem;\\n  --color-primary: #3498db;\\n}\\n\\nh1 {\\n  font-size: var(--font-heading-h1);\\n}\\n\\n.card-title {\\n  font-size: var(--font-heading-h1);\\n  color: var(--color-primary);\\n}\\n\\n.card {\\n  --font-heading-h1: 1.8rem;\\n}\\n\\n.card h1 {\\n  font-size: var(--font-heading-h1);\\n}"],[589,"A ready-made design system speeds development up, ensures consistency and reduces the cost of maintenance, but it limits flexibility and may not match a product\'s unique requirements. Your own components give full control over the design and the behaviour, but they call for more time and resources both to develop and to maintain. The choice depends on the deadlines, the budget, the specifics of the product and the team\'s long-term goals.","Introduction\\nThe choice between a ready-made design system and developing your own components is a strategic decision that affects development speed, the quality of the product and the cost of maintenance. Ready-made solutions such as Material UI, Ant Design or Bootstrap offer proven components and styles, while your own components let you create a unique user interface that matches the brand and the requirements exactly.\\nThe advantages of ready-made design systems\\nUsing a ready-made design system gives you a fast start: the components are already implemented, tested and documented. That reduces development time and lets the team concentrate on the business logic. Such systems also guarantee a consistent interface, since every component follows the same rules. Material UI, for example, provides ready-made buttons, forms, modal windows and theming, which makes building responsive interfaces easier.\\n\\nThe limitations of ready-made solutions\\nThe main disadvantage is limited flexibility. If a product calls for a non-standard design or specific behaviour, you end up having to override styles and extend the components, which can lead to conflicts and complicate the code. Ready-made systems are also often large, which affects performance if tree shaking is not used.\\nThe advantages of your own components\\nDeveloping your own components gives you full control over the design, the accessibility and the performance. That is ideal for products with a unique brand or complex requirements. You can create a component that fits a specific user scenario perfectly, for example, and optimize it for the particular needs.\\n\\nThe disadvantages of your own components\\nIt calls for considerable time and resources: every component has to be designed, implemented, tested and maintained. The risk of failing to meet accessibility standards and cross-browser compatibility also grows, if the team does not have enough experience.\\nConclusion\\nUsing a ready-made design system is justified for projects with limited deadlines and budget, where speed and consistency matter. Your own components are worth developing when a product calls for a unique design or a high degree of customization and the team is ready to invest in long-term maintenance. The optimal solution is often a hybrid approach: use a ready-made system as the basis and extend it with components of your own for the specific tasks.","// An example of using Material UI\\nimport Button from \'@mui/material/Button\';\\n\\nfunction App() {\\n  return <Button variant=\\"contained\\">Click me</Button>;\\n}\\n\\n// An example of a custom button component\\nfunction CustomButton({ children, onClick }) {\\n  return (\\n    <button onClick={onClick} style={{ padding: \'10px\', borderRadius: \'8px\' }}>\\n      {children}\\n    </button>\\n  );\\n}"],[590,"The main formats are: JPEG (photographs, lossy compression), PNG (graphics with transparency, lossless), WebP (modern, compresses better). The browser chooses a format through a `<source>` tag with a type attribute, or through the HTTP Accept header, which states the formats it supports. The server can serve WebP if the browser supports it.","The main image formats\\nJPEG is a lossy format, ideal for photographs and for complex images with smooth colour gradients. It gives good compression but does not support transparency. PNG is a lossless format that supports transparency and is suited to logos, icons and images with sharp edges. WebP is a modern format from Google that supports both lossy and lossless compression, as well as transparency. It usually gives a smaller file size at the same quality than JPEG and PNG.\\nHow the browser chooses a format\\nThe browser does not choose the format directly — it either receives the image by its URL, or the server decides which format to serve on the basis of the `Accept` header the browser sends in its HTTP request. Modern browsers, for example, state `image/webp` in that header, and the server can serve the WebP version. For static sites the `<picture>` tag with several sources is more often used:\\n\\nThe browser chooses the first `<source>` it supports, and if none of them is suitable it uses the `<img>` as the fallback. This lets you use WebP for modern browsers and JPEG for older ones.\\nPractical use\\nIn web development it is important to optimize your images: use WebP for most cases, JPEG for photographs without transparency, PNG for graphics with transparency. You can also use the `loading=\\"lazy\\"` attribute for deferred loading and `srcset` for responsive sizes.\\nConclusion: understanding the formats and the mechanism of choice helps you build fast, efficient sites, saving bandwidth and improving the user experience.","<picture>\\n  <source srcset=\\"image.webp\\" type=\\"image/webp\\">\\n  <source srcset=\\"image.jpg\\" type=\\"image/jpeg\\">\\n  <img src=\\"image.jpg\\" alt=\\"A description\\">\\n</picture>"],[591,"em and rem are relative units in CSS. em depends on the font size of the parent element, while rem depends on the font size of the root element (html). That means em can compound with nesting, whereas rem is always stable. Use rem for global spacing and sizes, and em for elements that should scale relative to their context.","The main difference\\nThe units `em` and `rem` in CSS are both relative, but they are tied to different base values. `em` is computed relative to the font size of the parent element, while `rem` is computed relative to the font size of the root `<html>` element. This key difference determines their behaviour in complex structures.\\nHow em works\\nWhen you set a property such as `font-size: 1.5em`, the browser multiplies the parent\'s current font size by 1.5. If the parent has `font-size: 16px`, the element will get 24px. With several levels of nesting the `em` value can compound: if the parent has 1.5em and the child also has 1.5em, the final size will be 1.5 * 1.5 = 2.25 times the base. That can lead to unexpected results, but it also lets you create components that scale in proportion to their context.\\nHow rem works\\n`rem` always refers to the font size of the root element, which is 16px by default in most browsers. That makes `rem` predictable and convenient for global settings such as spacing, block sizes or typography. `margin: 2rem`, for example, will always be 32px, regardless of nesting.\\nA practical example\\n\\nIn this example `.child-em` will get 30px, since 1.5 is multiplied by the parent\'s 20px, while `.child-rem` gets 24px, since 1.5 is multiplied by the root\'s 16px.\\nWhen to use which\\n\\n- `rem` — for global sizes: spacing, container widths, font sizes that have to be the same across the whole page.\\n- `em` — for local scaling: the sizes of buttons or icons, for example, which have to depend on the font size of the parent block.\\nConclusion\\nUnderstanding the difference between `em` and `rem` helps you create flexible, predictable styles. Use `rem` for uniformity and ease of maintenance, and `em` when you need responsiveness inside a component. This is especially important when developing large projects with a great many nested elements.","html { font-size: 16px; }\\n.parent { font-size: 20px; }\\n.child-em { font-size: 1.5em; } /* 30px */\\n.child-rem { font-size: 1.5rem; } /* 24px */"],[592,"A home-grown component library is maintained through consistent principles for its API and its styles. Theming is implemented by means of CSS variables or a theme context, and customization through props and slots. It is important to document the components and to version the changes.","The basics of maintaining a component library\\nMaintaining a home-grown component library calls for strict discipline: a consistent API, versioning, documentation and testing. Without those, the components quickly turn into chaos, where every developer adds props and styles of their own. The key principle is encapsulation: a component should manage its own logic and styles while providing flexible extension points.\\nTheming through CSS variables\\nThe simplest and most powerful way of theming is to use CSS variables (custom properties). They are inherited and can be overridden at the container level, which lets you change the theme without a rebuild. Define the variables on the root element, for example, and redefine them for the dark theme.\\n\\nIn JavaScript you can toggle the `data-theme` attribute on the root element. This works in any framework and needs no additional libraries.\\nCustomization through props and slots\\nThe customization of components is achieved through props (variants, sizes, states) and through slots/children for inserting arbitrary content. A button can take a `variant` and a `size` prop, for example, and also let the icon be overridden through its children.\\n\\nFor deeper customization you can use render props or context, but that complicates the API. It is better to keep to the principle that 80% of cases are covered by props and the rest by CSS variables and slots.\\nPractical recommendations\\n\\n- Version your changes by SemVer, so that consumers can upgrade safely.\\n- Write documentation with examples for every theme and variant.\\n- Use visual regression tests (Storybook + Chromatic, for example).\\n- Avoid rigid dependencies on a particular framework, if multi-platform support is planned.\\nConclusion\\nMaintaining a component library is a balance between flexibility and stability. Theming through CSS variables and customization through props is the standard approach, and it is easy to maintain and to extend. Apply it if you need a reusable design system across several projects.",":root {\\n  --color-primary: #3498db;\\n  --spacing-unit: 8px;\\n}\\n\\n[data-theme=\\"dark\\"] {\\n  --color-primary: #2980b9;\\n}\\n\\n.button {\\n  background: var(--color-primary);\\n  padding: calc(var(--spacing-unit) * 2);\\n}\\n\\nfunction Button({ variant = \'primary\', size = \'md\', children }) {\\n  return <button className={\'btn btn--\' + variant + \' btn--\' + size}>{children}</button>;\\n}"],[593,"Functions are blocks of code that carry out a particular task. They help avoid the duplication of code, make it simpler to maintain and make the program more understandable. A function can take data (parameters) and return a result. For example:\\n\\n`function greet(name) {\\nreturn `Hello, ${name}!`;\\n}\\nconsole.log(greet(\\"Ivan\\")); // Will print: Hello, Ivan!`","A function in programming is a named block of code that carries out a particular task. Functions help make code more convenient, structured and easy to maintain.\\nWhat functions are for:\\n\\n- Reuse: Code written once in a function can be called many times.\\n\\n- Readability: Functions divide a program into logical parts, which makes understanding the code simpler.\\n\\n- Modularity: The program becomes more organised and scales more easily.\\n\\n- The elimination of duplication: Common tasks can be moved out into functions, so that you do not have to write them again.\\n\\nHow functions work:\\n\\n- The declaration of the function: Its name, its parameters (if they are needed) and the block of code to be carried out are stated.\\n\\n- The call of the function: The code inside the function is carried out when it is called.\\n\\nAn example in JavaScript:\\n\\nTypes of functions:\\n\\n- With parameters: They take input data (arguments) in order to carry out the task.\\n\\n- Without parameters: They carry out a task that requires no input data.\\n\\n- Functions that return a result: They use `return` to return a value.\\n\\n- Anonymous functions: They have no name and are often used as arguments or in callbacks.\\n\\nAn example of practical use:\\nYou are building a site where you have to calculate a discount for a customer:\\n\\nThe practical benefit:\\nFunctions make your code flexible and understandable. If the logic of calculating the discount changes, you will only have to update the function rather than the whole code of the site. They are the basic building block of any application.","// The declaration of the function\\nfunction sum(a, b) {\\n  return a + b;\\n}\\n// The call of the function\\nconst result = sum(3, 5);\\nconsole.log(result); // Will print: 8\\n\\nfunction greet(name) {\\n    return `Hello, ${name}!`;\\n}\\nconsole.log(greet(\\"Anna\\")); // Hello, Anna!\\n\\nfunction sayHello() {\\n       console.log(\\"Hello, world!\\");\\n}\\nsayHello();\\n\\nfunction square(num) {\\n       return num * num;\\n}\\nconsole.log(square(4)); // 16\\n\\nconst multiply = function(a, b) {\\n       return a * b;\\n};\\nconsole.log(multiply(2, 3)); // 6\\n\\nfunction calculateDiscount(price, discount) {\\n       return price - (price * discount) / 100;\\n}\\n\\nconst finalPrice = calculateDiscount(1000, 20);\\nconsole.log(`Final price: ${finalPrice} roubles`); // Final price: 800 roubles"],[594,"`this` in JavaScript is a keyword that refers to the current execution context. The context depends on where and how `this` is used:\\n\\n- In a method of an object, `this` points to that object.\\n\\n- In a function, `this` points by default to the global object (in strict mode — `undefined`).\\n\\n- In arrow functions, `this` is inherited from the outer context.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\n`const obj = {\\nname: \\"Ivan\\",\\ngreet() {\\nconsole.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);\\n},\\n};\\nobj.greet(); // Hello, Ivan!`","`this` is a special keyword in JavaScript that determines which object the current code refers to. Its value is established at the moment the function is called rather than at the moment it is defined, which sometimes causes confusion.\\nHow `this` works:\\n\\n- Inside the methods of an object:\\nIf the function has been called as a method of an object, `this` points to that object.\\n\\n- In ordinary functions:\\nIf the function has been called in the global context (outside strict mode), `this` refers to the global object (`window` in the browser, `global` in Node.js).\\nIn strict mode (`\'use strict\'`) `this` will be `undefined`.\\n\\n- In constructors:\\nIn constructor functions `this` points to the object being created.\\n\\n- In arrow functions:\\nIn arrow functions `this` is inherited from the outer context where the function was defined.\\n\\nExamples of use and of problems:\\n\\n- The loss of the context:\\nWhen a method of an object is passed into a variable or a function, `this` loses its connection with the object.\\n\\nThe solution: Use `bind`, arrow functions or methods such as `.call()` and `.apply()`.\\n\\n- `this` in event handlers:\\nIn event handlers `this` refers to the element the handler is bound to.\\n\\n- `this` in classes:\\nIn the methods of a class `this` points to the instance of the class.\\n\\nConclusion\\nAn understanding of `this` helps you avoid typical errors in JavaScript and work correctly with objects, functions and methods. The use of arrow functions, strict mode and the binding of the context (`bind`) makes working with `this` more predictable.","const obj = {\\n  name: \\"Anna\\",\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);\\n  },\\n};\\nobj.greet(); // Hello, Anna!\\n\\nfunction showThis() {\\n  console.log(this);\\n}\\nshowThis(); // In the browser: window, in strict mode: undefined\\n\\nfunction Person(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n}\\nconst user = new Person(\\"Ivan\\");\\nconsole.log(user.name); // Ivan\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  name: \\"Peter\\",\\n  greet: () => {\\n    console.log(this.name);\\n  },\\n};\\nobj.greet(); // undefined, since an arrow function\'s this is taken from the global context\\n\\nconst obj = {\\n  name: \\"Sergey\\",\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(this.name);\\n  },\\n};\\nconst greet = obj.greet;\\ngreet(); // undefined\\n\\nconst boundGreet = obj.greet.bind(obj);\\nboundGreet(); // Sergey\\n\\nconst button = document.querySelector(\\"button\\");\\nbutton.addEventListener(\\"click\\", function () {\\n  console.log(this); // The button that was clicked\\n});\\n\\nclass User {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n  greet() {\\n    console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);\\n  }\\n}\\nconst user = new User(\\"Maria\\");\\nuser.greet(); // Hello, Maria!"],[595,"A prototype is an object from which another object can inherit properties and methods. In JavaScript every object has a hidden `[[Prototype]]` property, which points to its prototype. That lets you organise inheritance, so as not to duplicate code and in order to reuse common methods. If a method is not found in an object, for example, JavaScript looks for it in the prototype.\\nAn example:\\n\\n`const animal = { eats: true };\\nconst dog = Object.create(animal);\\nconsole.log(dog.eats); // true`","In JavaScript objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects. This process happens through a mechanism called prototypal inheritance. A prototype is an object linked to another object through the hidden `[[Prototype]]` property (or `__proto__` in older browsers).\\nHow does prototypal inheritance work?\\nWhen you access a property of an object:\\n\\n- JavaScript first looks for it in the object itself.\\n\\n- If the property is not found, the search continues in its prototype.\\n\\n- If the property is in the prototype, it is used.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nWhy does the prototype matter?\\n\\n- Saving memory: Common methods and properties are held in a single object (the prototype) rather than copied for every instance.\\n\\n- Extensibility: You can add new methods to the prototype, and they will immediately become available to every object that inherits that prototype.\\n\\nCreating prototypes\\nThrough `Object.create()`\\nYou explicitly state which object will be the prototype.\\n\\nThrough constructor functions\\nConstructor functions automatically link the created object with the prototype through the `prototype` property.\\n\\nThrough classes\\nClasses in JavaScript are a convenient wrapper over prototypal inheritance.\\n\\nAn example of the search for a property in the chain of prototypes:\\n\\nPractical application\\nPrototypes are often used to create hierarchies of objects, for example:\\n\\n- In web development — the `Array` or `Object` methods (`push`, `map`, `toString`, for example) are defined in their prototypes.\\n\\n- In large applications — to inherit common functions between objects.\\n\\nThe prototype is the foundation of the inheritance system in JavaScript, which lets you create flexible and economical structures of objects. A knowledge of this concept helps you understand more deeply how the built-in objects and classes in JavaScript work.","const animal = {\\n  eats: true,\\n  walk() {\\n    console.log(\\"Walking\\");\\n  }\\n};\\nconst dog = Object.create(animal); // We create the object dog with the prototype animal\\ndog.barks = true;\\nconsole.log(dog.eats); // true (inherited from animal)\\ndog.walk();           // \\"Walking\\" (a method from the prototype animal)\\nconsole.log(dog.barks); // true (dog\'s own property)\\n\\nconst animal = { eats: true };\\nconst rabbit = Object.create(animal);\\nconsole.log(rabbit.eats); // true\\n\\nfunction Animal(name) {\\n  this.name = name;\\n}\\nAnimal.prototype.eats = true;\\nconst dog = new Animal(\\"Dog\\");\\nconsole.log(dog.eats); // true\\n\\nclass Animal {\\n  constructor(name) {\\n    this.name = name;\\n  }\\n  walk() {\\n    console.log(`${this.name} is walking`);\\n  }\\n}\\nconst cat = new Animal(\\"Cat\\");\\ncat.walk(); // \\"Cat is walking\\"\\n\\nconst grandparent = { heritage: \\"ancient\\" };\\nconst parent = Object.create(grandparent);\\nparent.legacy = \\"modern\\";\\nconst child = Object.create(parent);\\nconsole.log(child.heritage); // \\"ancient\\" (from grandparent)\\nconsole.log(child.legacy);   // \\"modern\\" (from parent)"],[596,"Event delegation is the approach in which an event is handled on the parent element rather than on every child element it relates to. That works thanks to event bubbling: an event raised on a child element rises up the DOM tree as far as the parent. This method lets you reduce the number of event handlers and makes managing them simpler.\\nAn example:\\n\\n`document.getElementById(\\"parent\\").addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\nif (event.target.tagName === \\"BUTTON\\") {\\nconsole.log(`Button pressed: ${event.target.textContent}`);\\n}\\n});`","In JavaScript an event raised on one element can \\"bubble\\" up the DOM tree, reaching its ancestors. Event delegation uses this property in order to handle events for a multitude of child elements by means of a single handler on the parent element.\\nWhat is event delegation for?\\n\\n- The optimisation of performance\\nIf you have many child elements (a list of 100 items, for example), adding a handler to every element creates an excessive load. Delegation lets you use a single handler.\\n\\n- The dynamic addition of elements\\nWhen new elements are added to the DOM dynamically, with delegation you do not have to add handlers to those elements by hand.\\n\\nHow does it work?\\n\\n- We add an event handler to the parent element.\\n\\n- Inside the handler we check `event.target`, in order to determine on which child element the event happened.\\n\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe advantages of delegation\\n\\n- Fewer handlers: A saving of resources, since one handler replaces a multitude.\\n\\n- Support for dynamic elements: It works even with elements added after the page has loaded.\\n\\nThe limitations of delegation\\n\\n- Not every event bubbles: The `focus` and `blur` events, for example, do not support bubbling. For them you can use `focusin` and `focusout`, which do bubble.\\n\\n- An increase in the complexity of the code: You have to write additional logic to check on which element the event happened.\\n\\nAn extended example: delegation in a list\\n\\nHow is this connected with modern libraries?\\nIn libraries such as React or Vue, event delegation is built into their implementation. They handle events automatically at the level of the virtual DOM, which minimises the addition of superfluous handlers.\\nEvent delegation is a tool for working with events in the DOM effectively. It is especially useful when you have to manage a large number of elements or create new ones dynamically.","<div id=\\"menu\\">\\n  <button>Button 1</button>\\n  <button>Button 2</button>\\n  <button>Button 3</button>\\n</div>\\n<script>\\n  const menu = document.getElementById(\\"menu\\");\\n  menu.addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\n    if (event.target.tagName === \\"BUTTON\\") {\\n      alert(`You pressed: ${event.target.textContent}`);\\n    }\\n  });\\n<\/script>\\n\\n<ul id=\\"list\\">\\n  <li>Item 1</li>\\n  <li>Item 2</li>\\n  <li>Item 3</li>\\n</ul>\\n<script>\\n  const list = document.getElementById(\\"list\\");\\n  list.addEventListener(\\"click\\", (event) => {\\n    if (event.target.tagName === \\"LI\\") {\\n      console.log(`You selected: ${event.target.textContent}`);\\n    }\\n  });\\n  // Let us add a new item dynamically\\n  const newItem = document.createElement(\\"li\\");\\n  newItem.textContent = \\"Item 4\\";\\n  list.appendChild(newItem);\\n<\/script>"],[597,"The async and defer attributes of the <script> tag are used to control how and when the browser loads and executes JavaScript. async loads the script asynchronously and executes it immediately after it has loaded, without waiting for the whole page to finish loading. defer also loads the script asynchronously, but it postpones its execution until the HTML document has fully loaded, which prevents the rendering of the page from being blocked.","When adding scripts to a page it matters to take into account how they can affect performance and the user experience. By default, when the browser meets a `<script>` tag, it suspends the rendering of the page in order to load and execute the script, which can slow down the display of the content.\\nThe `async` attribute lets the browser load the script asynchronously, which means that loading the script does not block the rendering of the page. As soon as the script has loaded, it is executed immediately, even if the HTML document has not yet fully loaded.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe `defer` attribute, for its part, also loads the script asynchronously, but it postpones its execution until the whole HTML document has been loaded. That lets you guarantee that the script is executed only after the page has fully loaded.\\nAn example:\\n\\nThe use of these attributes lets you optimise the loading time and improve the user experience, especially on pages with a large number of scripts or in the presence of heavy JavaScript frameworks.","<script src=\\"script.js\\" async><\/script>\\n\\n<script src=\\"script.js\\" defer><\/script>"],[598,"The Shadow DOM creates an isolated DOM tree for a web component, which is hidden from the rest of the document.\\nThe pluses:\\n\\n- It isolates the styles and the markup.\\n\\n- It makes reuse simpler.\\n\\n- It encapsulates the logic.","The Shadow DOM is a technology that lets you create a \\"shadow\\" DOM inside an element, ensuring the isolation of its content and its styles from the main document. That lets developers build web components that can contain their own styles and elements without affecting other parts of the page. The Shadow DOM makes elements more independent and reusable.\\nWhen you create a Shadow DOM for an element — by means of the `attachShadow()` method, for example — a new context is created that does not inherit the styles and the scripts of the outer document. Here is an example of code that demonstrates the creation of a Shadow DOM:\\n\\nThe advantages of the Shadow DOM:\\n\\n- The isolation of the styles: The styles inside the Shadow DOM do not conflict with the global styles, which makes it ideal for developing components.\\n\\n- Encapsulation: Everything inside the Shadow DOM is hidden from the outer surroundings, which reduces the likelihood of conflicts and errors.\\n\\n- Reusability: Components with a Shadow DOM can easily be reused in different parts of an application, ensuring consistency and making maintenance simpler.\\n\\nThe Shadow DOM is thus a powerful tool for developing web components, which provides encapsulation and protection against conflicts of styles.","class MyElement extends HTMLElement {    \\n  constructor() {        \\n    super();        \\n    const shadow = this.attachShadow({ mode: \'open\' });        \\n    shadow.innerHTML = `            \\n      <style>                \\n        p {                    \\n          color: blue;                \\n        }            \\n      </style>            \\n      <p>Hello from Shadow DOM!</p>        \\n    `;    \\n  } \\n} \\n\\ncustomElements.define(\'my-element\', MyElement);"],[599,"HTML5 provides various input formats, such as `<input type=\\"email\\">`, `<input type=\\"url\\">` and `<input type=\\"number\\">`, which automatically check the data entered against particular criteria. Built-in validation happens automatically when the form is submitted, and the browser notifies the user of errors if the data does not match the format. Custom validation, on the other hand, lets developers create their own rules for checking data by means of JavaScript, which gives more control and flexibility.","HTML5 introduced a multitude of new input types and improved the mechanism for validating data in forms. That lets developers build more interactive and convenient web applications with less time spent writing their own code.\\nInput formats in HTML5:\\nSome of the new input types include:\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"email\\">`: Checks that the value entered has the email format (user@example.com, for example).\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"url\\">`: Checks that the value entered matches the URL format (https://example.com, for example).\\n\\n- `<input type=\\"number\\">`: Restricts the input to numbers only and can include attributes for setting a range (`min` and `max`, for example).\\n\\nBuilt-in validation:\\nHTML5 provides built-in validation of forms, which happens automatically on submission. If the data does not match the requirements established (an incorrect email format, for example), the browser will show an error message and prevent the form from being submitted. For example:\\n\\nIf the user enters an invalid email, the browser will notify them of it.\\nCustom validation:\\nCustom validation lets developers add their own rules for checking data. That is done by means of JavaScript and events such as `submit` or `input`. For example, you can create a check based on a particular condition:\\n\\nA comparison:\\n\\n- Built-in validation: The automatic checking of data against the types given, without the need to write additional code.\\n\\n- Custom validation: Lets you create more complex and specific checks that cannot be carried out by the standard means.\\n\\nBoth approaches can be used together in order to ensure the best user experience and raise the quality of the data entered.","<form>    \\n  <label for=\\"email\\">Email:</label>    \\n  <input type=\\"email\\" id=\\"email\\" required>    \\n  <button type=\\"submit\\">Submit</button> \\n</form>\\n\\nconst form = document.querySelector(\'form\'); \\nform.addEventListener(\'submit\', function(event) {    \\n  const email = document.getElementById(\'email\').value;    \\n  if (!email.endsWith(\'@example.com\')) {        \\n    alert(\'The email must end with @example.com\');        \\n    event.preventDefault(); // prevents the form from being submitted    \\n  } \\n});"],[600,"`<template>` is a special HTML element that lets you create fragments of code that are not displayed on the page until they are activated by means of JavaScript. The `<slot>` element is used in custom elements to define the place where content passed to the element can be inserted. These elements help developers build more flexible and reusable components that can change their content dynamically depending on the context.","HTML5 introduced the `<template>` and `<slot>` elements, which make creating dynamic interfaces and custom elements substantially simpler. These tools help developers manage a page\'s content effectively and build reusable components.\\nThe `<template>` element:\\nThis element is used to define a fragment of HTML that will not be displayed on the page until it is activated. That is useful for creating dynamic content without displaying it in the DOM directly. An example:\\n\\nTo use this template, you can obtain it by means of JavaScript and clone it:\\n\\nThe `<slot>` element:\\nThis element is used inside custom elements to indicate the places where the user\'s content can be inserted. That lets you create flexible components that can take different kinds of content. An example of a custom element using a slot:\\n\\nIn this example the content with the `slot=\\"header\\"` attribute will be inserted into the heading, and the remaining content into the main paragraph.\\nApplication:\\nThe use of `<template>` and `<slot>` lets developers create powerful and reusable components that can be configured dynamically depending on the context. That makes development more effective and makes managing the content on pages simpler.\\nThese tools thus help create cleaner, more organised and more effective code, which makes them important for modern web applications.","<template id=\\"myTemplate\\">    \\n  <div class=\\"item\\">A list item</div> \\n</template>\\n\\nconst template = document.getElementById(\'myTemplate\').content; \\nconst clone = document.importNode(template, true); \\ndocument.body.appendChild(clone);\\n\\n<template id=\\"myComponent\\">    \\n  <div>        \\n    <h1><slot name=\\"header\\"></slot></h1>        \\n    <p><slot></slot></p>    \\n  </div> \\n</template> \\n\\n<my-component>    \\n  <span slot=\\"header\\">The heading</span>    \\n  This content will be inserted into the main slot. \\n</my-component>"]]');export{e as default};